          </cluster>
        </cluster>
        <register>
          <name>CLK_SELECT</name>
          <description>Clock Selection for Power Mode Components</description>
          <addressOffset>0x2000</addressOffset>
          <resetMask>0x000300FF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CLK_PWR_DIV</name>
              <description>clk_pwr is generated by dividing the CLK_PWR_MUX selection by (CLK_PWR_DIV+1).</description>
              <bitRange>[7:0]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CLK_PWR_MUX</name>
              <description>Selects a source for the clock used by power control components.  Note that not all products support all clock sources.  Selecting a clock source that is not supported will result in undefined behavior.  It takes four cycles of the originally selected clock to switch away from it.  Do not disable the original clock during this time.</description>
              <bitRange>[17:16]</bitRange>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>IMO</name>
                  <description>IMO - Internal R/C Oscillator</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>IHO</name>
                  <description>IHO - Internal High-speed Oscillator</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>RSVD</name>
                  <description>N/A</description>
                  <value>2</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>BACKUP</name>
      <version>1.3</version>
      <description>SRSS Backup Registers</description>
      <baseAddress>0x42220000</baseAddress>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0</offset>
        <size>65536</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
        <register>
          <name>CTL</name>
          <description>Control</description>
          <addressOffset>0x0</addressOffset>
          <resetMask>0xFF0F3708</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>WCO_EN</name>
              <description>Watch-crystal oscillator (WCO) enable.  If there is a write in progress when this bit is cleared, the WCO will be internally kept on until the write completes.
After enabling the WCO software must wait until STATUS.WCO_OK=1 before configuring any component that depends on clk_lf/clk_bak, like for example RTC or WDTs.  Follow the procedure in BACKUP_RTC_RW to access this bit.</description>
              <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CLK_SEL</name>
              <description>Clock select for RTC clock</description>
              <bitRange>[10:8]</bitRange>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>WCO</name>
                  <description>Watch-crystal oscillator input, available in Active, DeepSleep, Hibernate, and XRES.  For products with an independent vbackup supply, it can continue operating in OFF w/Backup mode.</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>ALTBAK</name>
                  <description>This allows to use the LFCLK selection as an alternate backup domain clock. Note that LFCLK is only available in Active and DeepSleep power modes.
Note that LFCLK clock glitches can propagate into the backup logic when the clock is stopped.  For this reason, if the WCO or ILO is intended as the clock source then choose it directly instead of routing through LFCLK.</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>ILO</name>
                  <description>Internal Low frequency Oscillator, available in Active, DeepSleep, Hibernate, and XRES.  For products with an independent vbackup supply, it can continue operating in OFF w/Backup mode.
For Hibernate operation CLK_ILO0_CONFIG. ILO_BACKUP must be set.  If there are multiple ILO, this is ILO0.</description>
                  <value>2</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>LPECO_PRESCALER</name>
                  <description>Low-power external crystal oscillator prescaler output, available in Active, DeepSleep, Hibernate, and XRES.  For products with an independent vbackup supply, it can continue operating in OFF w/Backup mode.</description>
                  <value>3</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>PILO</name>
                  <description>Precision internal low-speed oscillator, available in Active, DeepSleep, Hibernate, and XRES.  For products with an independent vbackup supply, it can continue operating in OFF w/Backup mode.</description>
                  <value>4</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PRESCALER</name>
              <description>N/A</description>
              <bitRange>[13:12]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WCO_BYPASS</name>
              <description>Configures the WCO for different board-level connections to the WCO pins.  For example, this can be used to connect an external watch crystal oscillator instead of a watch crystal.   In all cases, the two related GPIO pins (WCO input and output pins) must be configured as analog connections using GPIO registers, and they must be hooked at the board level as described below.  Configure this field before enabling the WCO, and do not change this setting when WCO_EN=1.
0: Watch crystal.  Connect a 32.768 kHz watch crystal between WCO input and output pins.
1: Clock signal, either a square wave or sine wave.  See PRESCALER field for connection information.</description>
              <bitRange>[16:16]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RSVD</name>
              <description>N/A</description>
              <bitRange>[18:17]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>VBACKUP_MEAS</name>
              <description>Connect vbackup supply to the vbackup_meas output for measurement by an ADC attached to amuxbusa_adft_vddd.  The vbackup_meas signal is scaled to 10 percent of vbackup, so it is within the supply range of the ADC.</description>
              <bitRange>[19:19]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>EN_CHARGE_KEY</name>
              <description>When set to 3C, the supercap charger circuit is enabled.  Any other code disables the supercap charger.  THIS CHARGING CIRCUIT IS FOR A SUPERCAP ONLY AND CANNOT SAFELY CHARGE A BATTERY.  DO NOT WRITE THIS KEY WHEN VBACKUP IS CONNECTED TO A BATTERY. Enabling this supercap charger with a depleted supercap can cause a temporary IR drop on the power rail supplying the logic in the backup domain.  During cold boot, FW can determine that this condition could exist by seeing that any backup domain register has been reset. The charging time is 2.2*R*C, where R=100Kohm. So for a 100uF supercap, FW should wait for the charging time of 22seconds before issuing BACKUP.RESET and then enabling the WCO/PILO/ILO clock source and expecting data in BREGs to be retained.</description>
              <bitRange>[31:24]</bitRange>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>RTC_RW</name>
          <description>RTC Read Write register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x8</addressOffset>
          <resetMask>0x00000003</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>READ</name>
              <description>Read bit
When this bit is set the RTC registers will be copied to user registers and frozen so that a coherent RTC value can safely be read. The RTC will keep on running.
Do not set the read bit if the RTC is still busy with a previous update (see RTC_BUSY bit) or if the Write bit is set. Do not set the Read bit at the same time that the Write bit is cleared.</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WRITE</name>
              <description>Write bit
Only when this bit is set can the RTC registers be written to (otherwise writes are ignored). This bit cannot be set if the RTC is still busy with a previous update (see RTC_BUSY bit) or if the Read bit is set or getting set.
The user writes to the RTC user registers, when the Write bit is cleared by the user then the user registers content is copied to the actual RTC registers.
Only user RTC registers that were written to will get copied, others will not be affected.
When the SECONDS field is updated then TICKS will also be reset (WDT is not affected).

Do not set the Write bit if the RTC is still busy with a previous update (see RTC_BUSY).  Do not set the Write bit at the same time that the Read bit is cleared. FW should NOT permit DeepSleep with a pending WRITE transaction.</description>
              <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CAL_CTL</name>
          <description>Oscillator calibration for absolute frequency</description>
          <addressOffset>0xC</addressOffset>
          <resetMask>0xB003007F</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CALIB_VAL</name>
              <description>Calibration value for absolute frequency (at a fixed temperature).  Each step causes 128 ticks to be added or removed each hour.  Effectively that means that each step is 1.085ppm (= 128/(60*60*32,768)) when CAL_COMP_PER_MIN is set at default value.
Positive values 0x01-0x3c (1..60) add pulses, negative values remove pulses, thus giving a range of +/-65.1 ppm (limited by 60 minutes per hour, not the range of this field) when CAL_COMP_PER_MIN is set at default value .

Calibration is performed hourly, starting at 59 minutes and 59 seconds, and applied as 64 ticks every 30 seconds until there have been 2*CALIB_VAL adjustments when CAL_COMP_PER_MIN is set at default value .</description>
              <bitRange>[5:0]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CALIB_SIGN</name>
              <description>Calibration sign:
0= Negative sign: remove pulses (it takes more clock ticks to count one second)
1= Positive sign: add pulses (it takes less clock ticks to count one second)</description>
              <bitRange>[6:6]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CAL_COMP_PER_MIN</name>
              <description>Select how many time calibration is performed per minute  per step of 64 , each time a 64 step is added or substracted one unit 2/4/8/16*CALIB_VAL is substracted.</description>
              <bitRange>[17:16]</bitRange>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>2</name>
                  <description>Calibration of 64 each is performed twice per minute for 2*CALIB_VAL per hour</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>4</name>
                  <description>Calibration of 64 each is performed four times per minute for 4*CALIB_VAL per hour</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>8</name>
                  <description>Calibration of 64 each is performed eight  times per minute for 8*CALIB_VAL per hour</description>
                  <value>2</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>16</name>
                  <description>Calibration of 64 each is performed sixteen times per minute for 16*CALIB_VAL per hour</description>
                  <value>3</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CAL_SEL</name>
              <description>Select calibration wave output signal</description>
              <bitRange>[29:28]</bitRange>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>CAL512</name>
                  <description>512Hz wave, not affected by calibration setting (not supported for 50/60Hz input clock: CTL.PRESCALER!=0)</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>RSVD</name>
                  <description>N/A</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>CAL2</name>
                  <description>2Hz wave, includes the effect of the calibration setting, (not supported for 50/60Hz input clock: CTL.PRESCALER!=0)</description>
                  <value>2</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>CAL1</name>
                  <description>1Hz wave, includes the effect of the calibration setting (supported for all input clocks)</description>
                  <value>3</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CAL_OUT</name>
              <description>Output enable for wave signal for calibration and allow CALIB_VAL to be written.</description>
              <bitRange>[31:31]</bitRange>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>STATUS</name>
          <description>Status</description>
          <addressOffset>0x10</addressOffset>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetMask>0x00000005</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RTC_BUSY</name>
              <description>Pending RTC write</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WCO_OK</name>
              <description>Obsolete.  Use WCO_STATUS.WCO_OK for future designs.</description>
              <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>RTC_TIME</name>
          <description>Calendar Seconds, Minutes, Hours, Day of Week</description>
          <addressOffset>0x14</addressOffset>
          <resetValue>0x01000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0x075F3F3F</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RTC_SEC</name>
              <description>Calendar seconds, 0-59</description>
              <bitRange>[5:0]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RTC_MIN</name>
              <description>Calendar minutes, 0-59</description>
              <bitRange>[13:8]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RTC_HOUR</name>
              <description>Calendar hours, value depending on 12/24HR mode
0=24HR: [20:16]=0-23
1=12HR: [20]:0=AM, 1=PM, [19:16]=1-12</description>
              <bitRange>[20:16]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CTRL_12HR</name>
              <description>Select 12/24HR mode: 1=12HR, 0=24HR</description>
              <bitRange>[22:22]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RTC_DAY</name>
              <description>Calendar Day of the week, 1-7
It is up to the user to define the meaning of the values, but 1=Monday is recommended</description>
              <bitRange>[26:24]</bitRange>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>RTC_DATE</name>
          <description>Calendar Day of Month, Month,  Year</description>
          <addressOffset>0x18</addressOffset>
          <resetValue>0x00000101</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0x007F0F1F</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RTC_DATE</name>
              <description>Calendar Day of the Month, 1-31
Automatic Leap Year Correction</description>
              <bitRange>[4:0]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RTC_MON</name>
              <description>Calendar Month, 1-12</description>
              <bitRange>[11:8]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RTC_YEAR</name>
              <description>Calendar year, 0-99</description>
              <bitRange>[22:16]</bitRange>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>ALM1_TIME</name>
          <description>Alarm 1 Seconds, Minute, Hours, Day of Week</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1C</addressOffset>
          <resetValue>0x01000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0x879FBFBF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ALM_SEC</name>
              <description>Alarm seconds, 0-59</description>
              <bitRange>[5:0]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ALM_SEC_EN</name>
              <description>Alarm second enable: 0=ignore, 1=match</description>
              <bitRange>[7:7]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ALM_MIN</name>
              <description>Alarm minutes, 0-59</description>
              <bitRange>[13:8]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ALM_MIN_EN</name>
              <description>Alarm minutes enable: 0=ignore, 1=match</description>
              <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ALM_HOUR</name>
              <description>Alarm hours, value depending on 12/24HR mode
24HR: [4:0]=0-23
12HR: [4]:0=AM, 1=PM, [3:0]=1-12</description>
              <bitRange>[20:16]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ALM_HOUR_EN</name>
              <description>Alarm hour enable: 0=ignore, 1=match</description>
              <bitRange>[23:23]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ALM_DAY</name>
              <description>Alarm Day of the week, 1-7
It is up to the user to define the meaning of the values, but 1=Monday is recommended</description>
              <bitRange>[26:24]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ALM_DAY_EN</name>
              <description>Alarm Day of the Week enable: 0=ignore, 1=match</description>
              <bitRange>[31:31]</bitRange>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>ALM1_DATE</name>
          <description>Alarm 1 Day of Month, Month</description>
          <addressOffset>0x20</addressOffset>
          <resetValue>0x00000101</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0x80008F9F</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ALM_DATE</name>
              <description>Alarm Day of the Month, 1-31
Leap Year corrected</description>
              <bitRange>[4:0]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ALM_DATE_EN</name>
              <description>Alarm Day of the Month enable: 0=ignore, 1=match</description>
              <bitRange>[7:7]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ALM_MON</name>
              <description>Alarm Month, 1-12</description>
              <bitRange>[11:8]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ALM_MON_EN</name>
              <description>Alarm Month enable: 0=ignore, 1=match</description>
              <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ALM_EN</name>
              <description>Master enable for alarm 1.
0: Alarm 1 is disabled.  Fields for date and time are ignored.
1: Alarm 1 is enabled.  Alarm triggers whenever the new date and time matches all the enabled date and time fields, which can happen more than once depending on configuration.  If none of the date and time fields are enabled, then this alarm triggers once every second.</description>
              <bitRange>[31:31]</bitRange>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>ALM2_TIME</name>
          <description>Alarm 2 Seconds, Minute, Hours, Day of Week</description>
          <addressOffset>0x24</addressOffset>
          <resetValue>0x01000000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0x879FBFBF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ALM_SEC</name>
              <description>Alarm seconds, 0-59</description>
              <bitRange>[5:0]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ALM_SEC_EN</name>
              <description>Alarm second enable: 0=ignore, 1=match</description>
              <bitRange>[7:7]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ALM_MIN</name>
              <description>Alarm minutes, 0-59</description>
              <bitRange>[13:8]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ALM_MIN_EN</name>
              <description>Alarm minutes enable: 0=ignore, 1=match</description>
              <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ALM_HOUR</name>
              <description>Alarm hours, value depending on 12/24HR mode
24HR: [4:0]=0-23
12HR: [4]:0=AM, 1=PM, [3:0]=1-12</description>
              <bitRange>[20:16]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ALM_HOUR_EN</name>
              <description>Alarm hour enable: 0=ignore, 1=match</description>
              <bitRange>[23:23]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ALM_DAY</name>
              <description>Alarm Day of the week, 1-7
It is up to the user to define the meaning of the values, but 1=Monday is recommended</description>
              <bitRange>[26:24]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ALM_DAY_EN</name>
              <description>Alarm Day of the Week enable: 0=ignore, 1=match</description>
              <bitRange>[31:31]</bitRange>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>ALM2_DATE</name>
          <description>Alarm 2 Day of Month, Month</description>
          <addressOffset>0x28</addressOffset>
          <resetValue>0x00000101</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0x80008F9F</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ALM_DATE</name>
              <description>Alarm Day of the Month, 1-31
Leap Year corrected</description>
              <bitRange>[4:0]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ALM_DATE_EN</name>
              <description>Alarm Day of the Month enable: 0=ignore, 1=match</description>
              <bitRange>[7:7]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ALM_MON</name>
              <description>Alarm Month, 1-12</description>
              <bitRange>[11:8]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ALM_MON_EN</name>
              <description>Alarm Month enable: 0=ignore, 1=match</description>
              <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ALM_EN</name>
              <description>Master enable for alarm 2.
0: Alarm 2 is disabled.  Fields for date and time are ignored.
1: Alarm 2 is enabled.  Alarm triggers whenever the new date and time matches all the enabled date and time fields, which can happen more than once depending on configuration.  If none of the date and time fields are enabled, then this alarm triggers once every second.</description>
              <bitRange>[31:31]</bitRange>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>INTR</name>
          <description>Interrupt request register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x2C</addressOffset>
          <resetMask>0x00000007</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ALARM1</name>
              <description>Alarm 1 Interrupt</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ALARM2</name>
              <description>Alarm 2 Interrupt</description>
              <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CENTURY</name>
              <description>Century overflow interrupt</description>
              <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>INTR_SET</name>
          <description>Interrupt set request register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x30</addressOffset>
          <resetMask>0x00000007</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ALARM1</name>
              <description>Write with '1' to set corresponding bit in interrupt request register.</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToSet</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ALARM2</name>
              <description>Write with '1' to set corresponding bit in interrupt request register.</description>
              <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToSet</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CENTURY</name>
              <description>Write with '1' to set corresponding bit in interrupt request register.</description>
              <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToSet</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>INTR_MASK</name>
          <description>Interrupt mask register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x34</addressOffset>
          <resetMask>0x00000007</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ALARM1</name>
              <description>Mask bit for corresponding bit in interrupt request register.</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ALARM2</name>
              <description>Mask bit for corresponding bit in interrupt request register.</description>
              <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CENTURY</name>
              <description>Mask bit for corresponding bit in interrupt request register.</description>
              <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>INTR_MASKED</name>
          <description>Interrupt masked request register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x38</addressOffset>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetMask>0x00000007</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ALARM1</name>
              <description>Logical and of corresponding request and mask bits.</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ALARM2</name>
              <description>Logical and of corresponding request and mask bits.</description>
              <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CENTURY</name>
              <description>Logical and of corresponding request and mask bits.</description>
              <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>PMIC_CTL</name>
          <description>PMIC control register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x44</addressOffset>
          <resetValue>0xA0010000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xE001FF00</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>UNLOCK</name>
              <description>This byte must be set to 0x3A for PMIC to be disabled.  When the UNLOCK code is not present: writes to PMIC_EN field are ignored and the hardware ignores the value in PMIC_EN.  Do not change PMIC_EN in the same write cycle as setting/clearing the UNLOCK code; do these in separate write cycles.</description>
              <bitRange>[15:8]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>POLARITY</name>
              <description>N/A</description>
              <bitRange>[16:16]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PMIC_EN_OUTEN</name>
              <description>Output enable for the output driver in the PMIC_EN pad.
0: Output pad is tristate for PMIC_EN pin.  This can allow this pin to be used for another purpose. Tristate condition is kept only if the UNLOCK key (0x3A) is present
1: Output pad is enabled for PMIC_EN pin.</description>
              <bitRange>[29:29]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PMIC_ALWAYSEN</name>
              <description>Override normal PMIC controls to prevent accidentally turning off the PMIC by errant firmware.
0: Normal operation, PMIC_EN and PMIC_OUTEN work as described
1: PMIC_EN and PMIC_OUTEN are ignored and the output pad is forced enabled.
Note: This bit is a write-once bit until the next backup reset.</description>
              <bitRange>[30:30]</bitRange>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToSet</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PMIC_EN</name>
              <description>Enable for external PMIC that supplies vddd (if present).  This bit will only clear if UNLOCK was written correctly in a previous write operation and PMIC_ALWAYSEN=0.  When PMIC_EN=0, the system functions normally until vddd is no longer present (OFF w/Backup mode).  Firmware can set this bit, if it does so before vddd is actually removed.  This bit is also set by any RTC alarm or PMIC pin wakeup event regardless of UNLOCK setting.</description>
              <bitRange>[31:31]</bitRange>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>RESET</name>
          <description>Backup reset register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x48</addressOffset>
          <resetMask>0x80000000</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RESET</name>
              <description>Writing 1 to this register resets the backup logic.  Hardware clears it when the reset is complete.  After setting this register, firmware should confirm it reads as 0 before attempting to write other backup registers.</description>
              <bitRange>[31:31]</bitRange>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToSet</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>WCO_STATUS</name>
          <description>WCO Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x90</addressOffset>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetMask>0x00000004</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>WCO_OK</name>
              <description>Indicates that output has transitioned.</description>
              <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <dim>4</dim>
          <dimIncrement>4</dimIncrement>
          <name>BREG_SET0[%s]</name>
          <description>Backup register region 0</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1000</addressOffset>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BREG</name>
              <description>Backup memory that contains application-specific data.  Memory is retained on vbackup supply.</description>
              <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <dim>4</dim>
          <dimIncrement>4</dimIncrement>
          <name>BREG_SET1[%s]</name>
          <description>Backup register region 1</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1010</addressOffset>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BREG</name>
              <description>Backup memory that contains application-specific data.  Memory is retained on vbackup supply.</description>
              <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <dim>8</dim>
          <dimIncrement>4</dimIncrement>
          <name>BREG_SET2[%s]</name>
          <description>Backup register region 2</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1020</addressOffset>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BREG</name>
              <description>Backup memory that contains application-specific data.  Memory is retained on vbackup supply.</description>
              <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <dim>48</dim>
          <dimIncrement>4</dimIncrement>
          <name>BREG_SET3[%s]</name>
          <description>Backup register region 3</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1080</addressOffset>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BREG</name>
              <description>Backup memory that contains application-specific data.  Memory is retained on vbackup supply.</description>
              <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>CRYPTOLITE</name>
      <version>1.2</version>
      <description>N/A</description>
      <baseAddress>0x42230000</baseAddress>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0</offset>
        <size>65536</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
        <register>
          <name>CTL</name>
          <description>Control</description>
          <addressOffset>0x0</addressOffset>
          <resetValue>0x00001000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0x00003F00</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>P</name>
              <description>User/privileged access control:
'0': user mode.
'1': privileged mode.

This field is set with the user/privileged access control of the transaction that writes any (including possible memory holes in the IP aperture) of the CRYPTO component registers; i.e. the access control is inherited from the write transaction and not specified by the transaction write data.

All CRYPTO component master transactions use the P field for the user/privileged access control ('hprot[1]').</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>NS</name>
              <description>Secure/on-secure access control:
'0': secure.
'1': non-secure.

This field is set with the secure/non-secure access control of the transaction that writes  any (including possible memory holes in the IP aperture) of the CRYPTO component registers; i.e. the access control is inherited from the write transaction and not specified by the transaction write data.

All CRYPTO component master transactions use the NS field for the secure/non-secure access control ('hprot[4]').</description>
              <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PC</name>
              <description>Protection context.

This field is set with the protection context of the transaction that writes any (including possible memory holes in the IP aperture) of the CRYPTO component registers; i.e. the context is inherited from the write transaction and not specified by the transaction write data.

All CRYPTO component master transactions use the PC field for the protection context.</description>
              <bitRange>[7:4]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MS</name>
              <description>Master identifier of the cryptography IP. This is a design time configurable parameter.</description>
              <bitRange>[11:8]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SHA_SEL</name>
              <description>Selects SHA function for use.
0x0: SHA operation is disabled.
0x1: SHA-256 operation is enabled.
0x2: SHA-384 operation is enabled.
0x3: SHA-512 operation is enabled.</description>
              <bitRange>[13:12]</bitRange>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>STATUS</name>
          <description>Status</description>
          <addressOffset>0x4</addressOffset>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetMask>0x00000001</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BUSY</name>
              <description>Busy indication:
'0': IP not busy.
'1': IP busy (AES, SHA or VU functionality (TRNG functionality NOT included)).</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>AES_DESCR</name>
          <description>AES descriptor pointer</description>
          <addressOffset>0x40</addressOffset>
          <resetMask>0x00000000</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>PTR</name>
              <description>AES descriptor pointer. The descriptor points to a structure with 32-bit words:
Word 0: Pointer to a 128-bit AES key.
Word 1: Pointer to a 128-bit source/plaintext.
Word 2: Pointer to a 128-bit destination/ciphertext.

A write to this register automatically starts a 128-bit AES encryption in ECB mode. The write ONLY takes effect when the IP is NOT busy (STATUS.BUSY is '0'). The write will be made pending/blocked as long as the IP is busy.

Note: the pointers must be 4B aligned.

Note: HW updates this field when the AES engine is busy.</description>
              <bitRange>[31:2]</bitRange>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>VU_DESCR</name>
          <description>VU descriptor pointer</description>
          <addressOffset>0x80</addressOffset>
          <resetMask>0x00000000</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>PTR</name>
              <description>VU descriptor pointer. The descriptor points to a structure with 32-bit words:
Word 0: Control word. Specifies operand size in 32-bit word multiples.
- WORD[7:0]: Source operand 0 32-bit words (minus 1).
- WORD[15:8]: Source operand 1 32-bit words (minus 1).
- WORD[24:16]: Destination operand 32-bit words (minus 1).
- WORD[31:28]: Opcode. '0': multiplication (MUL), '1': addition (ADD), '2': subtraction (SUB), '3': exclusive or (XOR), '4': binary multiplication (XMUL), '5': logical shift right by 1 (LSR1), '6': logical shift left by 1 (LSL1), '7': logical shift right (LSR), '8': conditional syubtraction (COND_SUB). '9': move (MOV).
Word 1: Pointer to source 0.
Word 2: Pointer to source 1.
Word 3: Pointer to destination.

A write to this register automatically starts a VU operation. The write ONLY takes effect when the IP is NOT busy (STATUS.BUSY is '0'). The write will be made pending/blocked as long as the IP is busy.

Note: the pointers must be 4B aligned.

Note: HW updates this field when the VU engine is busy.</description>
              <bitRange>[31:2]</bitRange>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>SHA_DESCR</name>
          <description>SHA descriptor pointer</description>
          <addressOffset>0xC0</addressOffset>
          <resetMask>0x00000000</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>PTR</name>
              <description>SHA descriptor pointer. The descriptor points to a structure with 32-bit words:
For message schedule function:
Word 0: Control word.
- WORD0[28]: '0' for message schedule function.
Word 1: Pointer to 512 b message chunk (input).
Word 2: Pointer to 64 * 32 b word message schedule array (output).
For process function:
Word 0: Control word.
- WORD0[28]: '1' for process function.
Word 1: Pointer to 8 * 32 b word current hash value (input) and new hash value (output).
Word 2: Pointer to 64 * 32 b word message schedule array (input).

A write to this register automatically starts a SHA operation. The write ONLY takes effect when the IP is NOT busy (STATUS.BUSY is '0'). The write will be made pending/blocked as long as the IP is busy.

Note: the pointers must be 32-bit word aligned.

Note: HW updates this field when the SHA engine is busy. This register is used for SHA-256, SHA-384 and SHA-512 operation.</description>
              <bitRange>[31:2]</bitRange>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>INTR_ERROR</name>
          <description>Error interrupt</description>
          <addressOffset>0xF0</addressOffset>
          <resetMask>0x00000001</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BUS_ERROR</name>
              <description>AHB-Lite master interface bus error or ECC error. Note that the IP terminates its AES, SHA or VU functionality when it detects an error.

Note: The error is sticky. This allows SW to check for an error after a series of operations, rather than checking after every individual operation.</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>INTR_ERROR_SET</name>
          <description>Error interrupt set</description>
          <addressOffset>0xF4</addressOffset>
          <resetMask>0x00000001</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BUS_ERROR</name>
              <description>Write this field with '1' to set corresponding INTR_ERROR field to '1' (a write of '0' has no effect).</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToSet</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>INTR_ERROR_MASK</name>
          <description>Error interrupt mask</description>
          <addressOffset>0xF8</addressOffset>
          <resetMask>0x00000001</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BUS_ERROR</name>
              <description>Mask for corresponding field in INTR_ERROR register.</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>INTR_ERROR_MASKED</name>
          <description>Error interrupt masked</description>
          <addressOffset>0xFC</addressOffset>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetMask>0x00000001</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BUS_ERROR</name>
              <description>Logical and of corresponding INTR_ERROR and INTR_ERROR_MASK fields.</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>TRNG_CTL0</name>
          <description>TRNG control 0</description>
          <addressOffset>0x100</addressOffset>
          <resetValue>0x33030000</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0x33FFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>SAMPLE_CLOCK_DIV</name>
              <description>Specifies the clock divider that is used to sample oscillator data. This clock divider is wrt. The IP clock.
'0': sample clock is the IP clock.
'1': sample clock is the IP clock divided by 2.
...
'255': sample clock is the IP clock divided by 256.</description>
              <bitRange>[7:0]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RED_CLOCK_DIV</name>
              <description>Specifies the clock divider that is used to produce reduced bits.
'0': 1 reduced bit is produced for each sample.
'1': 1 reduced bit is produced for each 2 samples.
...
'255': 1 reduced bit is produced for each 256 samples.

The reduced bits are considered random bits and shifted into TRNG_RESULT.DATA.</description>
              <bitRange>[15:8]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>INIT_DELAY</name>
              <description>Specifies an initialization delay: number of removed/dropped samples before reduced bits are generated. This field should be programmed in the range [1, 255]. After starting the oscillators, at least the first 2 samples should be removed/dropped to clear the state of internal synchronizers. In addition, it is advised to drop at least the second 2 samples from the oscillators (to circumvent the semi-predictable oscillator startup behavior). This result in the default field value of '3'. Field encoding is as follows:
'0': 1 sample is dropped.
'1': 2 samples are dropped.
...
'255': 256 samples are dropped.

The INTR.INITIALIZED interrupt cause is set to '1', when the initialization delay is passed.</description>
              <bitRange>[23:16]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>VON_NEUMANN_CORR</name>
              <description>Specifies if the 'von Neumann corrector' is disabled or enabled:
'0': disabled.
'1': enabled.
The 'von Neumann corrector' post-processes the reduced bits to remove a '0' or '1' bias. The corrector operates on reduced bit pairs ('oldest bit, newest bit'):
'00': no bit is produced.
'01': '0' bit is produced (oldest bit).
'10': '1' bit is produced (oldest bit).
'11': no bit is produced.
Note that the corrector produces bits at a random pace and at a frequency that is 1/4 of the reduced bit frequency (reduced bits are processed in pairs, and half of the pairs do NOT produce a bit).</description>
              <bitRange>[24:24]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>FEEDBACK_EN</name>
              <description>Specifies if the feedback of the reducution state is enabled:
'0': Disabled.
'1': Enabled.

Note: This field is added in the 'mxcryptolite' IP to address CDT#337111, in which it was observed that the reduction state feedback reduces  the effectiveness of the 'von Neumann corrector'. The default value is '1' to provide backward compatibility.</description>
              <bitRange>[25:25]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>STOP_ON_AP_DETECT</name>
              <description>Specifies if TRNG functionality is stopped on an adaptive proportion test detection (when HW sets INTR_ERROR.TRNG_AP_DETECT to '1'):
'0': Functionality is NOT stopped.
'1': Functionality is stopped (TRNG_CTL1 fields are set to '0' by HW). The DAS bitstream is set to '0'.</description>
              <bitRange>[28:28]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>STOP_ON_RC_DETECT</name>
              <description>Specifies if TRNG functionality is stopped on a repetition count test detection (when HW sets INTR_ERROR.TRNG_RC_DETECT to '1'):
'0': Functionality is NOT stopped.
'1': Functionality is stopped (TRNG_CTL1 fields are set to '0' by HW). The DAS bitstream is set to '0'.</description>
              <bitRange>[29:29]</bitRange>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>TRNG_CTL1</name>
          <description>TRNG control 1</description>
          <addressOffset>0x104</addressOffset>
          <resetMask>0x0000003F</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RO11_EN</name>
              <description>FW sets this field to '1' to enable the ring oscillator with 11 inverters.</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RO15_EN</name>
              <description>FW sets this field to '1' to enable the ring oscillator with 15 inverters.</description>
              <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>GARO15_EN</name>
              <description>FW sets this field to '1' to enable the fixed Galois ring oscillator with 15 inverters.</description>
              <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>GARO31_EN</name>
              <description>FW sets this field to '1' to enable the programmable Galois ring oscillator with up to 31 inverters. The TRNG_GARO_CTL register specifies the programmable polynomial.</description>
              <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>FIRO15_EN</name>
              <description>FW sets this field to '1' to enable the fixed Fibonacci ring oscillator with 15 inverters.</description>
              <bitRange>[4:4]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>FIRO31_EN</name>
              <description>FW sets this field to '1' to enable the programmable Fibonacci ring oscillator with up to 31 inverters. The TRNG_FIRO_CTL register specifies the programmable polynomial.</description>
              <bitRange>[5:5]</bitRange>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>TRNG_STATUS</name>
          <description>TRNG status</description>
          <addressOffset>0x10C</addressOffset>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetMask>0x00000001</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>INITIALIZED</name>
              <description>Reflects the state of the true random number generator:
'0': Not initialized (TRNG_CTL0.INIT_DELAY has NOT passed).
'1': Initialized (TRNG_CTL0.INIT_DELAY has passed).</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>TRNG_RESULT</name>
          <description>TRNG result</description>
          <addressOffset>0x110</addressOffset>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetMask>0x00000000</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>DATA</name>
              <description>Generated 32-bit true random number. The INTR.DATA_AVAILABLE interrupt cause is activated when the number is generated.</description>
              <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>TRNG_GARO_CTL</name>
          <description>TRNG GARO control</description>
          <addressOffset>0x120</addressOffset>
          <resetValue>0x21F81910</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0x7FFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>POLYNOMIAL</name>
              <description>Polynomial for programmable Galois ring oscillator. The polynomial is represented WITHOUT the high order bit (this bit is always assumed '1'). The polynomial should be aligned such that the more significant bits (bit 30 and down) contain the polynomial and the less significant bits (bit 0 and up) contain padding '0's.

Note: Default value per GESC#113.</description>
              <bitRange>[30:0]</bitRange>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>TRNG_FIRO_CTL</name>
          <description>TRNG FIRO control</description>
          <addressOffset>0x124</addressOffset>
          <resetValue>0x696F0221</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0x7FFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>POLYNOMIAL</name>
              <description>Polynomial for programmable Fibonacci ring oscillator. The polynomial is represented WITHOUT the high order bit (this bit is always assumed '1'). The polynomial should be aligned such that the more significant bits (bit 30 and down) contain the polynomial and the less significant bits (bit 0 and up) contain padding '0's.

Note: Default value per GESC#113.</description>
              <bitRange>[30:0]</bitRange>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>TRNG_MON_CTL</name>
          <description>TRNG monitor control</description>
          <addressOffset>0x140</addressOffset>
          <resetValue>0x00000002</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0x00000303</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BITSTREAM_SEL</name>
              <description>Selection of the bitstream:
'0': DAS bitstream.
'1': RED bitstream.
'2': TR bitstream.
'3': Undefined.</description>
              <bitRange>[1:0]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>AP</name>
              <description>Adaptive proportion (AP) test enable:
'0': Disabled.
'1': Enabled.

On a AP detection, HW sets this field to '0' and sets INTR_ERROR.TRNG_AP_DETECT to '1.</description>
              <bitRange>[8:8]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RC</name>
              <description>Repetition count (RC) test enable:
'0': Disabled.
'1': Enabled.

On a RC detection, HW sets this field to '0' and sets INTR_ERROR.TRNG_RC_DETECT to '1.</description>
              <bitRange>[9:9]</bitRange>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>TRNG_MON_RC_CTL</name>
          <description>TRNG monitor RC control</description>
          <addressOffset>0x150</addressOffset>
          <resetValue>0x000000FF</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0x000000FF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CUTOFF_COUNT8</name>
              <description>Cutoff count (legal range is [1, 255]):
'0': Illegal.
'1': 1 repetition.
...
'255': 255 repetitions.</description>
              <bitRange>[7:0]</bitRange>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>TRNG_MON_RC_STATUS0</name>
          <description>TRNG monitor RC status 0</description>
          <addressOffset>0x158</addressOffset>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetMask>0x00000001</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BIT</name>
              <description>Current active bit value:
'0': '0'.
'1': '1'.

This field is only valid when TRNG_MON_RC_STATUS1.REP_COUNT is NOT equal to '0'.</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>TRNG_MON_RC_STATUS1</name>
          <description>TRNG monitor RC status 1</description>
          <addressOffset>0x15C</addressOffset>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetMask>0x000000FF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>REP_COUNT</name>
              <description>Number of repetitions of the current active bit counter:
'0': 0 repetitions.
...
'255': 255 repetitions.</description>
              <bitRange>[7:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>TRNG_MON_AP_CTL</name>
          <description>TRNG monitor AP control</description>
          <addressOffset>0x160</addressOffset>
          <resetValue>0xFFFFFFFF</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CUTOFF_COUNT16</name>
              <description>Cutoff count (legal range is [1, 65535]).
'0': Illegal.
'1': 1 occurrence.
...
'65535': 65535 occurrences.</description>
              <bitRange>[15:0]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WINDOW_SIZE</name>
              <description>Window size (minus 1) :
'0': 1 bit.
...
'65535': 65536 bits.</description>
              <bitRange>[31:16]</bitRange>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>TRNG_MON_AP_STATUS0</name>
          <description>TRNG monitor AP status 0</description>
          <addressOffset>0x168</addressOffset>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetMask>0x00000001</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BIT</name>
              <description>Current active bit value:
'0': '0'.
'1': '1'.

This field is only valid when TRNG_MON_AP_STATUS1.OCC_COUNT is NOT equal to '0'.</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>TRNG_MON_AP_STATUS1</name>
          <description>TRNG monitor AP status 1</description>
          <addressOffset>0x16C</addressOffset>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>OCC_COUNT</name>
              <description>Number of occurrences of the current active bit counter:
'0': 0 occurrences
...
'65535': 65535 occurrences</description>
              <bitRange>[15:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WINDOW_INDEX</name>
              <description>Counter to keep track of the current index in the window (counts from '0' to TRNG_MON_AP_CTL.WINDOW_SIZE to '0').</description>
              <bitRange>[31:16]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>INTR_TRNG</name>
          <description>TRNG interrupt</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1F0</addressOffset>
          <resetMask>0x0000000F</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>INITIALIZED</name>
              <description>This interrupt cause is activated (HW sets the field to '1') when the TRNG is initialized.</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DATA_AVAILABLE</name>
              <description>This interrupt cause is activated (HW sets the field to '1') when 32 bits of TRNG data becomes available in TRNG_RESULT.</description>
              <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>AP_DETECT</name>
              <description>This interrupt cause is activated (HW sets the field to '1') when the TRNG monitor detects an 'adaptive proportion' error.</description>
              <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RC_DETECT</name>
              <description>This interrupt cause is activated (HW sets the field to '1') when the TRNG monitor detects an 'repetition count' error.</description>
              <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>INTR_TRNG_SET</name>
          <description>TRNG Interrupt set</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1F4</addressOffset>
          <resetMask>0x0000000F</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>INITIALIZED</name>
              <description>SW writes a '1' to this field to set the corresponding field in interrupt request register.</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToSet</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DATA_AVAILABLE</name>
              <description>SW writes a '1' to this field to set the corresponding field in interrupt request register.</description>
              <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToSet</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>AP_DETECT</name>
              <description>SW writes a '1' to this field to set the corresponding field in interrupt request register.</description>
              <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToSet</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RC_DETECT</name>
              <description>SW writes a '1' to this field to set the corresponding field in interrupt request register.</description>
              <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToSet</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>INTR_TRNG_MASK</name>
          <description>TRNG Interrupt mask</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1F8</addressOffset>
          <resetMask>0x0000000F</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>INITIALIZED</name>
              <description>Mask bit for corresponding field in interrupt request register.</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DATA_AVAILABLE</name>
              <description>Mask bit for corresponding field in interrupt request register.</description>
              <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>AP_DETECT</name>
              <description>Mask bit for corresponding field in interrupt request register.</description>
              <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RC_DETECT</name>
              <description>Mask bit for corresponding field in interrupt request register.</description>
              <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>INTR_TRNG_MASKED</name>
          <description>TRNG Interrupt masked</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1FC</addressOffset>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetMask>0x0000000F</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>INITIALIZED</name>
              <description>Logical and of corresponding request and mask bits.</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DATA_AVAILABLE</name>
              <description>Logical and of corresponding request and mask bits.</description>
              <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>AP_DETECT</name>
              <description>Logical and of corresponding request and mask bits.</description>
              <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RC_DETECT</name>
              <description>Logical and of corresponding request and mask bits.</description>
              <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>MXCORDIC_1_1</name>
      <version>1.1</version>
      <description>N/A</description>
      <baseAddress>0x42240000</baseAddress>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0</offset>
        <size>65536</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
        <register>
          <name>CTL</name>
          <description>N/A</description>
          <addressOffset>0x0</addressOffset>
          <resetMask>0x80000000</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ENABLED</name>
              <description>Enables/Disables IP</description>
              <bitRange>[31:31]</bitRange>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>ID</name>
          <description>Module Identification Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x8</addressOffset>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0xDCB2C001</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>MOD_REV</name>
              <description>Module Revision Number - MOD_REV</description>
              <bitRange>[7:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MOD_TYPE</name>
              <description>Module Type - MOD_TYPE</description>
              <bitRange>[15:8]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MOD_NUMBER</name>
              <description>Module Number Value - MOD_NUMBER</description>
              <bitRange>[31:16]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>INTR</name>
          <description>Interrupt Cause Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x10</addressOffset>
          <resetMask>0x0000000C</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CDEOC</name>
              <description>CORDIC End of Calculation Event Flag - CDEOC. This bitfield will preserve interrupt cause status also when IP is disabled. By writing '0' to CTL.ENABLED bitfield will not be reset.</description>
              <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CDERR</name>
              <description>CORDIC Error Event Flag - CDERR. This bitfield will preserve interrupt cause status also when IP is disabled. By writing '0' to CTL.ENABLED bitfield will not be reset.</description>
              <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>INTR_SET</name>
          <description>Interrupt Set Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x14</addressOffset>
          <resetMask>0x0000000C</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CDEOC</name>
              <description>CORDIC Event Flag Set - CDEOC</description>
              <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToSet</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CDERR</name>
              <description>CORDIC Error Event Flag Set - CDERR</description>
              <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToSet</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>INTR_MASK</name>
          <description>Interrupt Mask Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x18</addressOffset>
          <resetMask>0x0000000C</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CDEOC</name>
              <description>CORDIC End of Calculation Interrupt Mask- CDEOC</description>
              <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CDERR</name>
              <description>CORDIC Error Interrupt Mask - CDERR</description>
              <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>INTR_MASKED</name>
          <description>Interrupt Masked Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1C</addressOffset>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetMask>0x0000000C</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CDEOC</name>
              <description>CORDIC End of Calculation Interrupt Masked- CDEOC</description>
              <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CDERR</name>
              <description>CORDIC Error Interrupt Masked - CDERR</description>
              <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>KEEP</name>
          <description>CORDIC Keep Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x40</addressOffset>
          <resetMask>0x000000E0</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>KEEPX</name>
              <description>Last X Result as Initial Data for New Calculation - KEEPX</description>
              <bitRange>[5:5]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>KEEPY</name>
              <description>Last Y Result as Initial Data for New Calculation - KEEPY</description>
              <bitRange>[6:6]</bitRange>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>KEEPZ</name>
              <description>Last Z Result as Initial Data for New Calculation - KEEPZ</description>
              <bitRange>[7:7]</bitRange>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CON</name>
          <description>CORDIC Control Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x44</addressOffset>
          <resetValue>0x00000062</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0x000007FE</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>MODE</name>
              <description>Operating Mode - MODE</description>
              <bitRange>[2:1]</bitRange>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>LINEAR</name>
                  <description>N/A</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>CIRCULAR</name>
                  <description>N/A</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>RSVD</name>
                  <description>N/A</description>
                  <value>2</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>HYPERBOLIC</name>
                  <description>N/A</description>
                  <value>3</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ROTVEC</name>
              <description>Rotation Vectoring Selection - ROTVEC</description>
              <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>VECTORING</name>
                  <description>N/A</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>ROTATION</name>
                  <description>N/A</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ST_MODE</name>
              <description>Start Method - ST_MODE</description>
              <bitRange>[4:4]</bitRange>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>AUTO</name>
                  <description>Operation starts with write into CORDZ register</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>ST</name>
                  <description>Operation starts with write into START_CMD register</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>X_USIGN</name>
              <description>Result Data Format for X in Circular Vectoring Mode - X_USIGN</description>
              <bitRange>[5:5]</bitRange>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>SIGNED</name>
                  <description>N/A</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>UNSIGNED</name>
                  <description>N/A</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MPS</name>
              <description>X and Y Magnitude Prescaler - MPS</description>
              <bitRange>[7:6]</bitRange>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>DIVIDE_BY_1</name>
                  <description>N/A</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>DIVIDE_BY_2</name>
                  <description>N/A</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>DIVIDE_BY_4</name>
                  <description>N/A</description>
                  <value>2</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>RSVD</name>
                  <description>N/A</description>
                  <value>3</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>N_ITER</name>
              <description>Number of CORDIC iterations divided by 4</description>
              <bitRange>[10:8]</bitRange>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>N_ITER_4</name>
                  <description>N/A</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>N_ITER_8</name>
                  <description>N/A</description>
                  <value>2</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>N_ITER_12</name>
                  <description>N/A</description>
                  <value>3</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>N_ITER_16</name>
                  <description>N/A</description>
                  <value>4</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>N_ITER_20</name>
                  <description>N/A</description>
                  <value>5</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>N_ITER_24</name>
                  <description>N/A</description>
                  <value>6</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>N_ITER_28</name>
                  <description>N/A</description>
                  <value>7</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CORDX</name>
          <description>CORDIC X Data Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x48</addressOffset>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFF00</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>DATA</name>
              <description>Initial X Parameter Data - DATA</description>
              <bitRange>[31:8]</bitRange>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CORDY</name>
          <description>CORDIC Y Data Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x4C</addressOffset>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFF00</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>DATA</name>
              <description>Initial Y Parameter Data - DATA</description>
              <bitRange>[31:8]</bitRange>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CORDZ</name>
          <description>CORDIC Z Data Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x50</addressOffset>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFF00</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>DATA</name>
              <description>Initial Z Parameter Data - DATA</description>
              <bitRange>[31:8]</bitRange>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CORRX</name>
          <description>CORDIC X Result Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x54</addressOffset>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFF00</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RESULT</name>
              <description>X Calculation Result - RESULT</description>
              <bitRange>[31:8]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CORRY</name>
          <description>CORDIC Y Result Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x58</addressOffset>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFF00</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RESULT</name>
              <description>Y Calculation Result - RESULT</description>
              <bitRange>[31:8]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CORRZ</name>
          <description>CORDIC Z Result Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x5C</addressOffset>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFF00</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RESULT</name>
              <description>Z Calculation Result - RESULT</description>
              <bitRange>[31:8]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>STAT</name>
          <description>CORDIC Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x60</addressOffset>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetMask>0x00000001</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BSY</name>
              <description>Busy Indication - BSY</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>START_CMD</name>
          <description>CORDIC Start Command Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x64</addressOffset>
          <resetMask>0x00000001</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ST</name>
              <description>Start Calculation Set - ST</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>DEBUG600</name>
      <version>1.2</version>
      <description>N/A</description>
      <baseAddress>0x42250000</baseAddress>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0</offset>
        <size>256</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
        <register>
          <name>VERSION</name>
          <description>IP Version Information</description>
          <addressOffset>0x0</addressOffset>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetMask>0x00000000</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>MINOR</name>
              <description>IP Minor Revision Number</description>
              <bitRange>[7:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MAJOR</name>
              <description>IP Major Revision Number</description>
              <bitRange>[15:8]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>SVGS</name>
      <version>1.0</version>
      <description>SVGS Registers</description>
      <baseAddress>0x42260000</baseAddress>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0</offset>
        <size>256</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
        <cluster>
          <name>SVGS_MAIN</name>
          <description>SVGS Protected Runtime Registers (non-privileged SW accessible)</description>
          <addressOffset>0x0</addressOffset>
          <register>
            <name>SVGS_CTL</name>
            <description>SVGS Control Register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x0</addressOffset>
            <resetValue>0x00000065</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x0000010F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>ENABLE</name>
                <description>'0101' = Disable SVGS.
All other values will enable SVGS. Note that HW will disable during DEEPSLEEP for power savings.</description>
                <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>FAULT_EN</name>
                <description>0 = Disable Fault generation
1 = A fault will be generated to the CPUSS fault handler for any enabled comparator.  Note that this fault will not be registered if the reset is not masked for the triggered comparator (reset takes precedence).</description>
                <bitRange>[8:8]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>SVGS_STATUS</name>
            <description>SVGS Status Register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x4</addressOffset>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetMask>0x0000031F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>UVC_CAPTURED</name>
                <description>1 = UVC (Under Voltage Comparison function) occurred since last reset</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>OVC_CAPTURED</name>
                <description>1 = OVC (Over Voltage Comparison function) occurred since last reset</description>
                <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>UVFC_CAPTURED</name>
                <description>1 = UVFC (Under Voltage (Filtered) Comparison function) occurred since last reset</description>
                <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>NVC_CAPTURED</name>
                <description>1 = NVC (Noise Voltage Comparison function) occurred since last reset</description>
                <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>HVC_CAPTURED</name>
                <description>1 = HVC (High Voltage Comparison function) occurred since last reset</description>
                <bitRange>[4:4]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EN_SEQ_ACTIVE</name>
                <description>1 = HW enable sequence is active. SW should check that this sequence has completed meaning SVGS is fully enabled before security critical operations.</description>
                <bitRange>[8:8]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SVGS_ENABLED</name>
                <description>1 = SVGS block enable sequence is complete and SVGS is functioning per loaded settings.
0 = Block is disabled, or in process of enable sequence.</description>
                <bitRange>[9:9]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>SVGS_CMP_CFG_RO</name>
            <description>SVGS Comparator Configuration</description>
            <addressOffset>0x8</addressOffset>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetValue>0x001F0000</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x0F1F0F0F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>UVC_EN</name>
                <description>1 = Enable UVC (Under Voltage Comparison function)</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>OVC_EN</name>
                <description>1 = Enable OVC (Over Voltage Comparison function)</description>
                <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>UVFC_EN</name>
                <description>1 = Enable UVFC (Under Voltage (Filtered) Comparison function)</description>
                <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>NVC_EN</name>
                <description>1 = Enable NVC (Noise Voltage Comparison function)</description>
                <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>UVC_PWR</name>
                <description>1 = UVC in full power else half power mode</description>
                <bitRange>[8:8]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>OVC_PWR</name>
                <description>1 = OVC in full power else half power mode</description>
                <bitRange>[9:9]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>UVFC_PWR</name>
                <description>1 = UVFC in full power else half power mode</description>
                <bitRange>[10:10]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>NVC_PWR</name>
                <description>1 = NVC in full power else half power mode</description>
                <bitRange>[11:11]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>UVC_MASK</name>
                <description>1 = UVC masked from affecting svgs_glitch_event_hv</description>
                <bitRange>[16:16]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>OVC_MASK</name>
                <description>1 = OVC masked from affecting svgs_glitch_event_hv</description>
                <bitRange>[17:17]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>UVFC_MASK</name>
                <description>1 = UVFC masked from affecting svgs_glitch_event_hv</description>
                <bitRange>[18:18]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>NVC_MASK</name>
                <description>1 = NVC masked from affecting svgs_glitch_event_hv</description>
                <bitRange>[19:19]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>HVC_MASK</name>
                <description>1 = HVC masked from affecting svgs_glitch_event_hv</description>
                <bitRange>[20:20]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SVGS_UVFC_TC</name>
                <description>Configures the time constant of the UVFC functionality. Each increment in setting sets increment (logarithmically equi-spaced) in time constant
0'h sets time constant 20ns
1'h sets time constant 24.4ns
2'h sets time constant 29.8ns
3'h sets time constant 36.4ns
4'h sets time constant 44.4ns
5'h sets time constant 54.3ns
6'h sets time constant 66.2ns
7'h sets time constant 81ns
8'h sets time constant 98.8ns
A'h sets time constant 147.3ns
B'h sets time constant 179.9ns
C'h sets time constant 219.7ns
D'h sets time constant 268.3ns
E'h sets time constant 327.5ns
F'h sets time constant 400ns</description>
                <bitRange>[27:24]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>SVGS_CMP_TRIP_RO</name>
            <description>SVGS Comparator trip-point configuration</description>
            <addressOffset>0xC</addressOffset>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetMask>0x0F3F033F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>SVGS_UVC_TRIP</name>
                <description>Configures the threshold of UVC functionality in steps of 6.2mV.
000000 = 760.00mV
000001 = 766.2mV
....
111111 = 1150mV</description>
                <bitRange>[5:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SVGS_OVC_TRIP</name>
                <description>Configures the threshold of OVC functionality in steps of 25mV.
00 = 1250mV
01 = 1275mV
10 = 1300mV
11 = 1325mV</description>
                <bitRange>[9:8]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SVGS_UVFC_TRIP</name>
                <description>Configures the threshold of UVFC functionality in steps of 6.2mV.
000000 = 760.00mV
000001 = 766.2mV
....
111111 = 1150mV</description>
                <bitRange>[21:16]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SVGS_NVC_TRIP</name>
                <description>Configures the threshold of NVC functionality in steps of 10mV.
0000 = 30mV
0001 = 40mV
....
1111 = 180mV</description>
                <bitRange>[27:24]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>SVGS_TRIM_RO</name>
            <description>SVGS Trim Register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x10</addressOffset>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetMask>0x00000F0F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>SVGS_VREG_TRIM</name>
                <description>Regulator output voltage trim</description>
                <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SVGS_IBIAS_TRIM</name>
                <description>Bias current output trim</description>
                <bitRange>[11:8]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>SVGS_INTR</name>
            <description>SVGS Interrupt Register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x20</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x0000001F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>UVC_GLITCH</name>
                <description>UVC Glitch detected interrupt</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>OVC_GLITCH</name>
                <description>OVC Glitch detected interrupt</description>
                <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>UVFC_GLITCH</name>
                <description>UVFC Glitch detected interrupt</description>
                <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>NVC_GLITCH</name>
                <description>NVC Glitch detected interrupt</description>
                <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>HVC_GLITCH</name>
                <description>HVC Glitch detected interrupt</description>
                <bitRange>[4:4]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>SVGS_INTR_SET</name>
            <description>SVGS Interrupt Set Register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x24</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x0000001F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>UVC_GLITCH</name>
                <description>Set interrupt for UVC_GLITCH interrupt.</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToSet</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>OVC_GLITCH</name>
                <description>Set interrupt for OVC_GLITCH interrupt.</description>
                <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToSet</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>UVFC_GLITCH</name>
                <description>Set interrupt for UVFC_GLITCH interrupt.</description>
                <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToSet</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>NVC_GLITCH</name>
                <description>Set interrupt for NVC_GLITCH interrupt.</description>
                <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToSet</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>HVC_GLITCH</name>
                <description>Set interrupt for HVC_GLITCH interrupt.</description>
                <bitRange>[4:4]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToSet</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>SVGS_INTR_MASK</name>
            <description>SVGS Interrupt Mask Register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x28</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x0000001F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>UVC_GLITCH</name>
                <description>Mask for UVC_GLITCH interrupt.</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>OVC_GLITCH</name>
                <description>Mask for OVC_GLITCH interrupt.</description>
                <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>UVFC_GLITCH</name>
                <description>Mask for UVFC_GLITCH interrupt.</description>
                <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>NVC_GLITCH</name>
                <description>Mask for NVC_GLITCH interrupt.</description>
                <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>HVC_GLITCH</name>
                <description>Mask for HVC_GLITCH interrupt.</description>
                <bitRange>[4:4]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>SVGS_INTR_MASKED</name>
            <description>SVGS Interrupt Masked Register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x2C</addressOffset>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetMask>0x0000001F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>UVC_GLITCH</name>
                <description>Logical and of corresponding request and mask bits.</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>OVC_GLITCH</name>
                <description>Logical and of corresponding request and mask bits.</description>
                <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>UVFC_GLITCH</name>
                <description>Logical and of corresponding request and mask bits.</description>
                <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>NVC_GLITCH</name>
                <description>Logical and of corresponding request and mask bits.</description>
                <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>HVC_GLITCH</name>
                <description>Logical and of corresponding request and mask bits.</description>
                <bitRange>[4:4]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
        </cluster>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>MXAES</name>
      <version>2.2</version>
      <description>N/A</description>
      <baseAddress>0x42270000</baseAddress>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0</offset>
        <size>8192</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
        <cluster>
          <name>UNMASKED_USER_RANGE</name>
          <description>AES command and configuration registers</description>
          <addressOffset>0x0</addressOffset>
          <register>
            <name>CFG</name>
            <description>AES configuration register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x0</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x00001F1F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>CALC_MODE</name>
                <description>Mode of AES calculation
This field selects the mode of operation.</description>
                <bitRange>[4:0]</bitRange>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>NOMODE</name>
                    <description>No calculation mode selected</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>CIPHER</name>
                    <description>Set the cipher mode (corresponds to an encryption or decryption operation)</description>
                    <value>3</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CRYPT_MODE</name>
                <description>Cryptographic mode
This field selects the key's length and key state location of the AES cryptographic engine</description>
                <bitRange>[12:8]</bitRange>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>NOMODE</name>
                    <description>No key size selection</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>AES128LO</name>
                    <description>Set AES mode using lower half 128-bit key</description>
                    <value>16</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>AES128HI</name>
                    <description>Set AES mode using higher half 128-bit key</description>
                    <value>19</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>AES256</name>
                    <description>Set AES mode using 256-bit key</description>
                    <value>22</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>CMD</name>
            <description>AES command register</description>
            <addressOffset>0xC</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x00000003</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>START_ENC</name>
                <description>Launch an AES encryption operation
Setting this bit launches an encryption. Any write to this bit is ignored if data is not valid and/or key for the selected key length is not valid. The bit is cleared only by hardware as soon as the flag BSY is cleared.</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>NENC</name>
                    <description>No encryption operation is launched</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ENC</name>
                    <description>An encryption operation is launched</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>START_DEC</name>
                <description>Launch an AES decryption operation
Setting this bit launches a decryption. Any write to this bit is ignored if data is not valid and/or key for the selected key length is not valid. The bit is cleared only by hardware as soon as the flag BSY is cleared.</description>
                <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>NDEC</name>
                    <description>No decryption operation is launched</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DEC</name>
                    <description>A decryption operation is launched</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>STAT</name>
            <description>AES status register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x14</addressOffset>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetValue>0x00002000</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x7F1FEF17</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>DATA</name>
                <description>AES data valid
This flag becomes valid when a complete 128-bit data block is written to the data registers.</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DNV</name>
                    <description>Data has not been completely loaded</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DV</name>
                    <description>Data is valid</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>AES_KEY_128LO</name>
                <description>AES 128 Low key valid</description>
                <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ALIV</name>
                    <description>128-bit AES key low has not been completely loaded</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ALV</name>
                    <description>128-bit AES key low is valid</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>AES_KEY_128HI</name>
                <description>AES 128 High key valid</description>
                <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>AHIV</name>
                    <description>128-bit AES key high has not been completely loaded</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>AHV</name>
                    <description>128-bit AES key high is valid</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>AES_KEY_256</name>
                <description>AES 256 Key valid</description>
                <bitRange>[4:4]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>A2IV</name>
                    <description>256-bit AES key has not been completely loaded</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>A2V</name>
                    <description>256-bit AES key is valid</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SEC_LEVEL_RR</name>
                <description>SEC_LEVEL reread bits</description>
                <bitRange>[11:8]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SEED</name>
                <description>Seeding status bit
Reading this bit gives the programmer the information if a seeding has been performed. Otherwise the software must write 4 words seed to the SEED_P register to seed the internal PRNG of the module. This bit is permanently set to '1' when the parameter NRNGA_INTEGRATION=1.</description>
                <bitRange>[13:13]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>SEED</name>
                    <description>Seeding already done</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>NO_SEED</name>
                    <description>Seeding has not been performed yet</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RDY</name>
                <description>Ready flag
The ready flag indicates whether the result of an operation is available. It is asserted as soon as a valid result is available. This flag is deasserted in one of the following cases: any bit in the CLEAR register is set, any data register is written, or any legal write to CFG register.</description>
                <bitRange>[14:14]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>AES_NRDY</name>
                    <description>Result is not ready to be read</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>AES_RDY</name>
                    <description>Result is valid and ready to be read</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BSY</name>
                <description>Busy flag
The busy flag indicates whether the AES engine is busy or not. Attention this flag indicates whether an encryption or decryption is undergoing or not.</description>
                <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>NBSY</name>
                    <description>Module is not busy with an AES operation</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>BSY</name>
                    <description>Module is busy doing a cryptographic operation</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CALC_MODE_RR</name>
                <description>CALC_MODE reread bits</description>
                <bitRange>[20:16]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CRYPT_MODE_RR</name>
                <description>CRYPT_MODE reread bits</description>
                <bitRange>[28:24]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>START_ENC_RR</name>
                <description>START_ENC reread bit
This bit is set by hardware as soon as CMD.START_ENC is set by software. This bit is cleared by hardware as soon as STAT.BSY is cleared.</description>
                <bitRange>[29:29]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>START_DEC_RR</name>
                <description>START_DEC reread bit
This bit is set by hardware as soon as CMD.START_DEC is set by software. This bit is cleared by hardware as soon as STAT.BSY is cleared.</description>
                <bitRange>[30:30]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>CFG_2</name>
            <description>AES configuration register 2</description>
            <addressOffset>0x60</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x0000000F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>SEC_LEVEL</name>
                <description>Security level
This field defines the overall security level. This feature consists of randomizing the AES execution and increases the resistance against side channel attacks targeting a key extraction as well as the strength of the backward calculation against differential fault analysis. This feature has a significant impact on the AES performance. The default value 0x0 gives the least randomization level with least performance drop impact. The security level can be increased by setting any valid values from 0x1 to 0xA) to this field. Any value greater than 0xA is evaluated as 0xA.</description>
                <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>INTR</name>
            <description>AES interrupt cause register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x80</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x00000001</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>CALC_FINISHED</name>
                <description>Interrupt calculation finished
This cause bit is set to 1 by hardware when the AES engine is not busy anymore with a calculation (also notified by the STAT.BSY flag). This is the unique interrupt cause of the module. This interrupt is cleared by software by either writing a 1 or by module reset or with the CLEAR_ALL bit. The software clear overrules the hardware set in case both happen simultaneously.</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>CLEAR</name>
                    <description>Clear interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>CLEARED</name>
                    <description>No interrupt or interrupt cleared by software or module reset</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>INTR_SET</name>
            <description>AES interrupt set register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x84</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x00000001</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>CALC_FINISHED</name>
                <description>Interrupt set calculation finished
Interrupt set bit corresponding to interrupt cause bit. This bit is readable. Only the software can set it by writing 1.</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToSet</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>INTR_MASK</name>
            <description>AES interrupt mask register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x8C</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x00000001</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>CALC_FINISHED</name>
                <description>Interrupt mask calculation finished
The interrupt cause bit will only cause an interrupt when its mask bit is set.</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>INTR_MASKED</name>
            <description>AES masked interrupt register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x90</addressOffset>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetMask>0x00000001</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>CALC_FINISHED</name>
                <description>Masked interrupt calculation finished
Logical and of corresponding request and mask bits.</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>CLEAR</name>
            <description>AES clear register</description>
            <addressOffset>0xC0</addressOffset>
            <access>write-only</access>
            <resetMask>0x00000037</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>DATA</name>
                <description>Clear data</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>NCLR</name>
                    <description>Data keeps its value</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>CLR</name>
                    <description>Data clearing triggered</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>AES_KEY_128LO</name>
                <description>Clear AES 128 Low Key</description>
                <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>NCLR</name>
                    <description>128-bit AES key low keeps its value</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>CLR</name>
                    <description>128-bit AES key is cleared</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>AES_KEY_128HI</name>
                <description>Clear AES 128 High Key</description>
                <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>NCLR</name>
                    <description>128-bit AES key high keeps its value</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>CLR</name>
                    <description>128-bit AES key high is cleared</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>AES_KEY_256</name>
                <description>Clear AES 256 Key</description>
                <bitRange>[4:4]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>CLR</name>
                    <description>256-bit AES key is cleared if it is set in the STAT register. This clears
AES_KEY_128HI and LO keys</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>NCLR</name>
                    <description>256-bit AES key keeps its value</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CLEAR_ALL</name>
                <description>Clear all SFRs and AES internal registers
Setting this bit behaves as a module reset. It clears all AES keys, data,
all SFRs and all AES internal registers.</description>
                <bitRange>[5:5]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>NCLR</name>
                    <description>Clear is disabled</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>CLR</name>
                    <description>A clear all is triggered</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>BUS_COUNTER</name>
            <description>AES Bus counter register</description>
            <addressOffset>0xDC</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x000000FF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>CTR</name>
                <description>Key and data write counter
The counter is incremented on every bus write access of data or key. On Debug write access of key or data registers the counter is not incremented. It is reset on module reset but never reset otherwise. When it reaches 0xFF and a key or data bus write happens, it is incremented to 0x00 while the overflow is ignored.</description>
                <bitRange>[7:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>QCHAN_CTL</name>
            <description>AES Q-channel control register</description>
            <addressOffset>0xE0</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x00000003</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>QDENY_RESP</name>
                <description>Q-Channel QDENY Response Policy.
This policy bit provides SW flexibility to determine whether to assert qdeny or qaccept when qreq is asserted but the IP is not quiescent. Note: The RNG always accepts the request with a clock cycle delay. That is, the register does not have any effect and is a dummy register.</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACCEPT</name>
                    <description>A quiescence request is always accepted</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DENY</name>
                    <description>A quiescence request may be denied</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>QDENY_STATUS</name>
                <description>Q-Channel QDENY Status.
This status bit allows dynamic debug when a qdeny occurs.</description>
                <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>NODENY</name>
                    <description>No quiescence request was denied</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DENIED</name>
                    <description>A quiescence request was denied</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
        </cluster>
        <cluster>
          <name>MASKED_USER_RANGE</name>
          <description>AES data and key shares</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1000</addressOffset>
          <register>
            <name>SEED_U</name>
            <description>AES user seeding register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x0</addressOffset>
            <access>write-only</access>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>PRNG_SEED</name>
                <description>Seed for internal masking
Writing to this register triggers the internal seeding of the PRNG mask generator of AES. The content of this register is used to seed the AES mask generator in addition to the NRNGA internal noise source. It does not reset the PRNG, it actually adds up entropy to the entropy of the NRNGA internal noise source. This register is not readable however writable at any time also during STAT.BSY status.</description>
                <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>KEY_0_0</name>
            <description>AES share 0 word 0 key register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x100</addressOffset>
            <access>write-only</access>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>KEY</name>
                <description>AES key_x_y register (x for index of shares; y for index of word)</description>
                <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>KEY_0_1</name>
            <description>The key registers are write only and reads trigger a BusError. When the module is busy (STAT.BSY = BSY) writes to the register also trigger a BusError.</description>
            <addressOffset>0x10C</addressOffset>
            <access>write-only</access>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>KEY</name>
                <description>AES key_x_y register (x for index of shares; y for index of word)</description>
                <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>KEY_0_2</name>
            <description>AES share 0 word 2 key register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x118</addressOffset>
            <access>write-only</access>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>KEY</name>
                <description>AES key_x_y register (x for index of shares; y for index of word)</description>
                <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>KEY_0_3</name>
            <description>AES share 0 word 3 key register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x124</addressOffset>
            <access>write-only</access>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>KEY</name>
                <description>AES key_x_y register (x for index of shares; y for index of word)</description>
                <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>KEY_0_4</name>
            <description>AES share 0 word 4 key register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x130</addressOffset>
            <access>write-only</access>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>KEY</name>
                <description>AES key_x_y register (x for index of shares; y for index of word)</description>
                <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>KEY_0_5</name>
            <description>AES share 0 word 5 key register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x13C</addressOffset>
            <access>write-only</access>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>KEY</name>
                <description>AES key_x_y register (x for index of shares; y for index of word)</description>
                <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>KEY_0_6</name>
            <description>AES share 0 word 6 key register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x148</addressOffset>
            <access>write-only</access>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>KEY</name>
                <description>AES key_x_y register (x for index of shares; y for index of word)</description>
                <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
