                      <description>N/A</description>
                      <value>16</value>
                    </enumeratedValue>
                    <enumeratedValue>
                      <name>AC</name>
                      <description>AC trigger</description>
                      <value>17</value>
                    </enumeratedValue>
                    <enumeratedValue>
                      <name>DAC_PERIOD_TC</name>
                      <description>Period terminal count. Period divider value is programmed in DAC_PERIOD register</description>
                      <value>18</value>
                    </enumeratedValue>
                    <enumeratedValue>
                      <name>CMP_LEVEL</name>
                      <description>Use comparator output as Update Trigger: (Used only in Hysteretic Mode )
With DAC_VAL_A as high threshold and DAC_VAL_B as low threshold,
CMP 0 to 1 transition (voltage high) outputs DAC_VAL_B ...</description>
                      <value>19</value>
                    </enumeratedValue>
                    <enumeratedValue>
                      <name>CMP_LEVEL_INV</name>
                      <description>Use inverted comparator output as Update Trigger (Used only in Hysteretic Mode )
(e.g. in the case CMP_POLARITY is set)</description>
                      <value>20</value>
                    </enumeratedValue>
                  </enumeratedValues>
                </field>
                <field>
                  <name>DAC_MODE</name>
                  <description>DAC Operating Mode.  This setting may only be changed when the mode is disabled. See DAC_MODE_START.</description>
                  <bitRange>[12:10]</bitRange>
                  <enumeratedValues>
                    <enumeratedValue>
                      <name>BUFFERED</name>
                      <description>Single Value Buffered Mode </description>
                      <value>0</value>
                    </enumeratedValue>
                    <enumeratedValue>
                      <name>HYSTERETIC</name>
                      <description>Two Value Hysteretic Buffered Mode</description>
                      <value>1</value>
                    </enumeratedValue>
                    <enumeratedValue>
                      <name>SLOPE_RISING</name>
                      <description>Rising Slope Mode </description>
                      <value>2</value>
                    </enumeratedValue>
                    <enumeratedValue>
                      <name>SLOPE_FALLING</name>
                      <description>Falling Slope Mode  </description>
                      <value>3</value>
                    </enumeratedValue>
                    <enumeratedValue>
                      <name>SLOPE_TRIANGULAR</name>
                      <description>Triangular Slope Mode</description>
                      <value>4</value>
                    </enumeratedValue>
                    <enumeratedValue>
                      <name>LUT_FORWARD</name>
                      <description>LUT Forward Mode</description>
                      <value>5</value>
                    </enumeratedValue>
                    <enumeratedValue>
                      <name>LUT_REVERSE</name>
                      <description>LUT Reverse Mode</description>
                      <value>6</value>
                    </enumeratedValue>
                    <enumeratedValue>
                      <name>LUT_FWD_REV</name>
                      <description>LUT Forward Reverse Mode</description>
                      <value>7</value>
                    </enumeratedValue>
                  </enumeratedValues>
                </field>
                <field>
                  <name>DAC_CONT</name>
                  <description>DAC Continuous Mode (Only valid for Slope and LUT modes)
0: Slope/LUT operation is One-Shot, last output value is held
1: Slope/LUT operation is Continuous, operation is restarted when end value is reached</description>
                  <bitRange>[13:13]</bitRange>
                </field>
                <field>
                  <name>DAC_SKIP_TR_EN</name>
                  <description>Skip the first DAC hardware update trigger afer a mode is enabled.  Allows for a minimum of a full trigger period before the first hardware update occurs. This bit should only be set before operations begin. It should remain set during operation as well.

0: First trigger following DAC_MODE_EN updates the DAC value.
1: First trigger following DAC_MODE_EN is skipped.  Following trigger updates the DAC value.</description>
                  <bitRange>[14:14]</bitRange>
                </field>
                <field>
                  <name>DAC_CASCADE_EN</name>
                  <description>Cascade Enable.  Synchronize parameter update with previous CSG slice in the case of multi-phase operation.
0: Disabled.
1: Enabled. This CSG updates it's parameters on a DAC trigger only after the previous numbered CSG has updated it's parameters.</description>
                  <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
                </field>
                <field>
                  <name>DAC_PARAM_SYNC_EN</name>
                  <description>Parameter Sync Enable.
0: Disabled.  Always update parameters on DAC trigger (as defined by DAC_MODE)
1: Enabled.   Only update parameters on DAC trigger if DAC_PARAM_SYNC.READY is set.</description>
                  <bitRange>[16:16]</bitRange>
                </field>
                <field>
                  <name>DAC_STEP</name>
                  <description>Static value of 6 bit DAC step size. Step size is DAC_STEP +1 (default is '1') , range is 1 - 64, only valid in SLOPE/LUT modes.
NOTE: In LUT mode each step jumps to a new LUT value on WORD boundaries.
So for example:
DAC_STEP = 0  (default Step size of 1) advances the LUT address by 4 (LUT WORD boundary)
DAC_STEP = 1 (Step size of 2) advances the LUT address by 8, etc</description>
                  <bitRange>[25:20]</bitRange>
                </field>
                <field>
                  <name>DAC_DEGLITCH</name>
                  <description>DAC Deglitch Configuration.  Default is disabled.  If enabled, on any DAC value update,the CMP output is latched in it's current state for the number of cycles indicated.</description>
                  <bitRange>[30:28]</bitRange>
                  <enumeratedValues>
                    <enumeratedValue>
                      <name>DISABLED</name>
                      <description>N/A</description>
                      <value>0</value>
                    </enumeratedValue>
                    <enumeratedValue>
                      <name>ONE_CYCLE</name>
                      <description>N/A</description>
                      <value>1</value>
                    </enumeratedValue>
                    <enumeratedValue>
                      <name>TWO_CYCLES</name>
                      <description>N/A</description>
                      <value>2</value>
                    </enumeratedValue>
                    <enumeratedValue>
                      <name>THREE_CYCLES</name>
                      <description>N/A</description>
                      <value>3</value>
                    </enumeratedValue>
                    <enumeratedValue>
                      <name>FOUR_CYCLES</name>
                      <description>N/A</description>
                      <value>4</value>
                    </enumeratedValue>
                    <enumeratedValue>
                      <name>FIVE_CYCLES</name>
                      <description>N/A</description>
                      <value>5</value>
                    </enumeratedValue>
                    <enumeratedValue>
                      <name>SIX_CYCLES</name>
                      <description>N/A</description>
                      <value>6</value>
                    </enumeratedValue>
                    <enumeratedValue>
                      <name>SEVEN_CYCLES</name>
                      <description>N/A</description>
                      <value>7</value>
                    </enumeratedValue>
                  </enumeratedValues>
                </field>
              </fields>
            </register>
            <register>
              <name>DAC_PARAM_SYNC</name>
              <description>DAC Parameter Synchronized Update</description>
              <addressOffset>0x8</addressOffset>
              <resetMask>0x00000001</resetMask>
              <fields>
                <field>
                  <name>READY</name>
                  <description>Use this bit to synchronize the update of multiple DAC parameters.

If DAC_PARAM_SYNC_EN bit is '1', then set this bit after writes to all parameter registers (VAL_A, VAL_B, DAC_PERIOD, DAC_STEP) are complete to synchronize parameter update on a subsequent DAC trigger This allows for writes to multiple registers to be written before new parameters are updated to avoid partial updates.

This bit is cleared by HW on the parameter update event.

if DAC_PARAM_SYNC_EN is '0', this bit is ignored and parameters are updated on the following UPDATE trigger.

NOTE: This bit can be used in conjunction with DAC_CASCADE_EN to synchronize the parameter update of multiple CSG slices.</description>
                  <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                  <modifiedWriteValues>oneToSet</modifiedWriteValues>
                </field>
              </fields>
            </register>
            <register>
              <name>DAC_MODE_START</name>
              <description>DAC Mode Start</description>
              <addressOffset>0xC</addressOffset>
              <resetMask>0xC0000001</resetMask>
              <fields>
                <field>
                  <name>BUSY</name>
                  <description>Read only bit to indicate that a DAC MODE has been enabled and is currently in progress.
If FW_START is used to start the mode, then the FW_START bit is equivalent to this bit and low when the mode is completed or stopped.
If HW_START is used to start the mode, then this status bit goes high on the DAC_START_TR event, and low when the mode is completed or stopped.

NOTE: This read only bit refects the state of the DAC controller which is reset in DeepSleep and therefore this bit is not a retention bit.</description>
                  <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                  <access>read-only</access>
                </field>
                <field>
                  <name>HW_START</name>
                  <description>1: Wait for subsequent DAC_START_TR  to start the selected mode.
0: Stops the selected mode.  When this bit is cleared, the current DAC_VAL will not change unless it is over-written directly - or a new DAC mode is started.
This field is automatically reset to '0' on DAC_SLOPE_DONE event (unless DAC_CONT is set for continous mode).</description>
                  <bitRange>[30:30]</bitRange>
                </field>
                <field>
                  <name>FW_START</name>
                  <description>1: Starts the selected mode on this FW write.
0: Stops the selected mode.  When this bit is cleared, the current DAC_VAL will not change unless it is over-written directly - or a new DAC mode is started.
This field is automatically reset to '0' on DAC_SLOPE_DONE event (unless DAC_CONT is set for continous mode).</description>
                  <bitRange>[31:31]</bitRange>
                </field>
              </fields>
            </register>
            <register>
              <name>DAC_VAL_A</name>
              <description>DAC Vaue A Buffer</description>
              <addressOffset>0x10</addressOffset>
              <resetMask>0x000003FF</resetMask>
              <fields>
                <field>
                  <name>VALUE</name>
                  <description>DAC A Value, usage is mode dependent (see DAC_MODE)

NOTE: In LUT modes this is the Start Address of the LUT waveform in byte address format (e.g. 0, 4, 8, ... 1F8, 1FC)</description>
                  <bitRange>[9:0]</bitRange>
                </field>
              </fields>
            </register>
            <register>
              <name>DAC_VAL_B</name>
              <description>DAC Value B Buffer</description>
              <addressOffset>0x14</addressOffset>
              <resetMask>0x000003FF</resetMask>
              <fields>
                <field>
                  <name>VALUE</name>
                  <description>DAC B Value, usage is mode dependent (see DAC_MODE)

NOTE: In LUT modes this is the End Address of the LUT waveform in byte address format (e.g. 0, 4, 8, ... 1F8, 1FC)</description>
                  <bitRange>[9:0]</bitRange>
                </field>
              </fields>
            </register>
            <register>
              <name>DAC_PERIOD</name>
              <description>DAC Period Buffer</description>
              <addressOffset>0x18</addressOffset>
              <resetMask>0x000FFFF8</resetMask>
              <fields>
                <field>
                  <name>PERIOD_FRAC</name>
                  <description>5-bit Fractional Divide Value. Allows for fractional count in the range [0, 31/32]</description>
                  <bitRange>[7:3]</bitRange>
                </field>
                <field>
                  <name>PERIOD_INT</name>
                  <description>12-bit Integer Divide Value.
Integer count  by (1+PERIOD_INT). Allows for integer counts in the range [1, 4096].</description>
                  <bitRange>[19:8]</bitRange>
                </field>
              </fields>
            </register>
            <register>
              <name>DAC_VAL</name>
              <description>DAC FW write</description>
              <addressOffset>0x1C</addressOffset>
              <resetMask>0x000003FF</resetMask>
              <fields>
                <field>
                  <name>VALUE</name>
                  <description>Direct drive to DAC Hard IP for output.
When DAC_MODE_EN is '0', the value in this register is output.  A write to this register also STOPs the current DAC_MODE operation, and subsequent DAC triggers are ignored.</description>
                  <bitRange>[9:0]</bitRange>
                </field>
              </fields>
            </register>
            <register>
              <name>DAC_STATUS</name>
              <description>Read of Current DAC Value</description>
              <addressOffset>0x20</addressOffset>
              <access>read-only</access>
              <resetMask>0x000003FF</resetMask>
              <fields>
                <field>
                  <name>DAC_VAL</name>
                  <description>Read Only value of current digital DAC output</description>
                  <bitRange>[9:0]</bitRange>
                  <access>read-only</access>
                </field>
              </fields>
            </register>
            <register>
              <name>CMP_STATUS</name>
              <description>Read of Comparator Value</description>
              <addressOffset>0x24</addressOffset>
              <access>read-only</access>
              <resetMask>0x00000001</resetMask>
              <fields>
                <field>
                  <name>CMP_VAL</name>
                  <description>Read Only value of comparator output</description>
                  <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                  <access>read-only</access>
                </field>
              </fields>
            </register>
          </cluster>
          <cluster>
            <dim>3</dim>
            <dimIncrement>512</dimIncrement>
            <name>LUT_CFG[%s]</name>
            <description>DAC LUT waveform data</description>
            <addressOffset>0x400</addressOffset>
            <register>
              <dim>128</dim>
              <dimIncrement>4</dimIncrement>
              <name>LUT_DATA[%s]</name>
              <description>LUT Waveform Data</description>
              <addressOffset>0x0</addressOffset>
              <resetMask>0x00000000</resetMask>
              <fields>
                <field>
                  <name>DATA</name>
                  <description>DAC waveform</description>
                  <bitRange>[9:0]</bitRange>
                </field>
              </fields>
            </register>
          </cluster>
          <register>
            <name>CSG_CTRL</name>
            <description>CSG control register</description>
            <addressOffset>0xA00</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x00000F0F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>VDAC_OUT_SEL</name>
                <description>Select the DAC output for SAR conversion</description>
                <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DISABLED</name>
                    <description>Hi-Z (Disabled)</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DAC0</name>
                    <description>Connect DAC0 to ADC input</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DAC1</name>
                    <description>Connect DAC1 to ADC input</description>
                    <value>2</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DAC2</name>
                    <description>Connect DAC2 to ADC input</description>
                    <value>3</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DAC3</name>
                    <description>Connect DAC3 to ADC input</description>
                    <value>4</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DAC4</name>
                    <description>Connect DAC4 to ADC input</description>
                    <value>5</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DAC5</name>
                    <description>Connect DAC5 to ADC input</description>
                    <value>6</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DAC6</name>
                    <description>Connect DAC6 to ADC input</description>
                    <value>7</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DAC7</name>
                    <description>Connect DAC7 to ADC input</description>
                    <value>8</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DAC8</name>
                    <description>Connect DAC8 to ADC input</description>
                    <value>9</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CSG_CLK_DIV_BYP</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[8:8]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CSG_GRP_PWR_EN</name>
                <description>Individual Group Power Enables.
0: Slices 0-2
1: Slices 3-5
2: Slices 6-8</description>
                <bitRange>[11:9]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <dim>3</dim>
            <dimIncrement>4</dimIncrement>
            <name>CSG_GROUP_CFG[%s]</name>
            <description>CSG Group Configuration Register</description>
            <addressOffset>0xA04</addressOffset>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>RISING_EDGE_DELAY_BLANK</name>
                <description>Comparator Blank Clock Period. Choose the number of clock periods to blank starting at the positive edge of the Level Trigger. This is scaled by the Comparator Blank Clock Divider.
0 - 255 - Clock Period selection</description>
                <bitRange>[7:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>FALLING_EDGE_DELAY_BLANK</name>
                <description>Comparator Blank Clock Period. Choose the number of clock periods to blank starting at the negative edge of the Level Trigger. This is scaled by the Comparator Blank Clock Divider.
0 - 255 - Clock Period selection</description>
                <bitRange>[15:8]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CMP_GLITCH_PERIOD</name>
                <description>Comparator Glitch Width Count. Choose the number of output values equal to 0x1 in series to achieve an output filter of 0x1. This number also reflects the number of output value equal to 0x0 in series to achieve a an output filter of 0x0.</description>
                <bitRange>[23:16]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CLOCK_DIVIDER</name>
                <description>Comparator Blank Clock Divider. To add more range to the blanking period, the clock can be divided.</description>
                <bitRange>[27:24]</bitRange>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DIV_1</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DIV_2</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DIV_4</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>2</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DIV_8</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>3</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DIV_16</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>4</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DIV_32</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>5</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DIV_64</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>6</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DIV_128</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>7</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DIV_256</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>8</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DIV_512</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>9</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DIV_1024</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>10</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DIV_2048</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>11</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>HYSTERESIS_EN_BANK</name>
                <description>Enable the Hysteresis in Bank of CSGs
0x0 - disabled Hysteresis
0x1 - enable Hysteresis</description>
                <bitRange>[28:28]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>HYSTERESIS_SEL</name>
                <description>Hysteresis magnitude selection</description>
                <bitRange>[31:29]</bitRange>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>HYST_0MV</name>
                    <description>0 mV</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>HYST_12MV</name>
                    <description>12 mV</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>HYST_24MV</name>
                    <description>24 mV</description>
                    <value>2</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>HYST_36MV</name>
                    <description>36 mV</description>
                    <value>3</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>HYST_48MV</name>
                    <description>48 mV</description>
                    <value>4</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>HYST_60MV</name>
                    <description>60 mV</description>
                    <value>5</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>HYST_72MV</name>
                    <description>72 mV</description>
                    <value>6</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>HYST_84MV</name>
                    <description>84mV</description>
                    <value>7</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <dim>3</dim>
            <dimIncrement>4</dimIncrement>
            <name>CSG_OUT_REMAP_CFG[%s]</name>
            <description>CSG Output Remap Configuration</description>
            <addressOffset>0xA10</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x000006FF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>REMAP_CFG_SELECT</name>
                <description>Remap CSG Output Logical Translation. The CSG Comparator outputs act as the control bits of an 8:1 mux. This field is the 8 bits.</description>
                <bitRange>[7:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CMP_REMAP_EDGE_MODE</name>
                <description>Comparator signal mode for Interrupt generation and AC status</description>
                <bitRange>[10:9]</bitRange>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DISABLE</name>
                    <description>edge detection disabled. Interrupt disabled</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>RISING</name>
                    <description>Rising edge</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>FALLING</name>
                    <description>Falling edge</description>
                    <value>2</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>BOTH</name>
                    <description>Both rising and falling edges</description>
                    <value>3</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DAC_INTR</name>
            <description>DAC Interrupt register</description>
            <addressOffset>0xB00</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x0FFFFDFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>DAC_HW_START</name>
                <description>This DAC_MODE operation was started with a HW START trigger.</description>
                <bitRange>[8:0]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>DAC_SLOPE_DONE</name>
                <description>When SLOPE or LUT mode is enabled, this interrupt is set when the SLOPE (or LUT waveform) has completed.
This interrupt is also set periodically on the last value of a DAC SLOPE in continuous mode</description>
                <bitRange>[18:10]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>DAC_BUF_EMPTY</name>
                <description>When BUFFERED or HYSTERETIC mode is enabled, this interrupt is set when there is a DAC UPDATE trigger that transfers the buffered value(s) to active value(s) (see mode operation) and it means that the user may write new values to the buffer registers.</description>
                <bitRange>[27:19]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DAC_INTR_SET</name>
            <description>DAC Interrupt request set register</description>
            <addressOffset>0xB04</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x0FFFFDFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>DAC_HW_START</name>
                <description>Write with '1' to set corresponding bit in interrupt request register.</description>
                <bitRange>[8:0]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToSet</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>DAC_SLOPE_DONE</name>
                <description>Write with '1' to set corresponding bit in interrupt request register.</description>
                <bitRange>[18:10]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToSet</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>DAC_BUF_EMPTY</name>
                <description>Write with '1' to set corresponding bit in interrupt request register.</description>
                <bitRange>[27:19]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToSet</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DAC_INTR_MASK</name>
            <description>DAC Interrupt request mask</description>
            <addressOffset>0xB08</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x0FFFFDFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>DAC_HW_START</name>
                <description>Mask bit for corresponding bit in interrupt request register.</description>
                <bitRange>[8:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>DAC_SLOPE_DONE</name>
                <description>Mask bit for corresponding bit in interrupt request register.</description>
                <bitRange>[18:10]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>DAC_BUF_EMPTY</name>
                <description>Mask bit for corresponding bit in interrupt request register.</description>
                <bitRange>[27:19]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DAC_INTR_MASKED</name>
            <description>DAC Interrupt request masked</description>
            <addressOffset>0xB0C</addressOffset>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetMask>0x0FFFFDFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>DAC_HW_START</name>
                <description>Logical and of corresponding request and mask bits.</description>
                <bitRange>[8:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>DAC_SLOPE_DONE</name>
                <description>Logical and of corresponding request and mask bits.</description>
                <bitRange>[18:10]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>DAC_BUF_EMPTY</name>
                <description>Logical and of corresponding request and mask bits.</description>
                <bitRange>[27:19]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>CMP_INTR</name>
            <description>CMP Interrupt register</description>
            <addressOffset>0xB10</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x00000FFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>CMP</name>
                <description>This CMP(n) state has generated an interrupt request (see CSG_SLICE.CMP_CFG.CMP_EDGE_MODE)</description>
                <bitRange>[8:0]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CMP_REMAP</name>
                <description>This CMP_REMAP(n) state has generated an interrupt request (see CSG_SLICE.CSG_OUT_REMAP_CFG.CMP_REMAP_EDGE_MODE)</description>
                <bitRange>[11:9]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>CMP_INTR_SET</name>
            <description>CMP Interrupt request set register</description>
            <addressOffset>0xB14</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x00000FFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>CMP</name>
                <description>Write with '1' to set corresponding bit in interrupt request register.</description>
                <bitRange>[8:0]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToSet</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CMP_REMAP</name>
                <description>Write with '1' to set corresponding bit in interrupt request register.</description>
                <bitRange>[11:9]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToSet</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>CMP_INTR_MASK</name>
            <description>CMP Interrupt request mask</description>
            <addressOffset>0xB18</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x00000FFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>CMP</name>
                <description>Mask bit for corresponding bit in interrupt request register.</description>
                <bitRange>[8:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CMP_REMAP</name>
                <description>Mask bit for corresponding bit in interrupt request register.</description>
                <bitRange>[11:9]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>CMP_INTR_MASKED</name>
            <description>CMP Interrupt request masked</description>
            <addressOffset>0xB1C</addressOffset>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetMask>0x00000FFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>CMP</name>
                <description>Logical and of corresponding request and mask bits.</description>
                <bitRange>[8:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CMP_REMAP</name>
                <description>Logical and of corresponding request and mask bits.</description>
                <bitRange>[11:9]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>VDAC_OUT_BLANK</name>
            <description>Blanking control for DAC Observability</description>
            <addressOffset>0xB20</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x0000001F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>BLANK_CNT</name>
                <description>This field enables blanking iin CLK_CSG cycles of the Comparator output when one of the DACs in a CSG SLICE is connected as an input for SAR conversion  (See CTRL.VDAC_OUT_SEL)
Disabled: BLANK_CNT = 0
Enabled : BLANK_CNT is between 1 and 31

When the DAC output is first connected to the SAR sampler input, the DAC may have a short transient (50ns). If the DAC is also an active input to the Comparator, this transient may cause false transitions on the Comparator output.  To mitigate this, the Comparator output is latched for the duration of the BLANK_CNT value.   This 5-bit value is the number of clock cycles to blank (latch) the Comparator output.  This count is in CLK_CSG cycles as programmed in the MXPERI divider.  The recommended value is 50ns blanking.
Examples are:
CLK_CSG @40MHz, use 2 cycles
CLK_CSG @80MHz, use 4 cycles
CLK_CSG @120MHz, use 8 cycles</description>
                <bitRange>[4:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>CSG_SYNC</name>
            <description>CSG Sync Enable register</description>
            <addressOffset>0xB30</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x00000001</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>SYNC_EN</name>
                <description>Risk Mitigation only.  By default the CSG Hard IP and comparator output is synchronous to CLK_CSG.  When this bit is set to '1', the CSG comparator output is double synced.</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <dim>9</dim>
            <dimIncrement>4</dimIncrement>
            <name>CSG_AROUTEMUX_SEL[%s]</name>
            <description>CSG AROUTE Mux Select Bits</description>
            <addressOffset>0xB40</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x0000000F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>AROUTE_SEL</name>
                <description>Aroute Mux Select. Its 4:1 Mux with 1-hot select</description>
                <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>AIN_A</name>
                    <description>Analog input A (each Mux is defined in SAS)</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>AIN_B</name>
                    <description>Analog input B (each Mux is defined in SAS)</description>
                    <value>2</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>AIN_C</name>
                    <description>Analog input C (each Mux is defined in SAS)</description>
                    <value>4</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>AIN_D</name>
                    <description>Analog input D (each Mux is defined in SAS)</description>
                    <value>8</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
        </cluster>
        <cluster>
          <name>ACTRLR</name>
          <description>Autonomnous Controller</description>
          <addressOffset>0xD0000</addressOffset>
          <register>
            <name>CTRL</name>
            <description>Control register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x0</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x80000000</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>ENABLED</name>
                <description>0: MCPASS IP disabled - All mision logic and status is reset, all analog controls are disabled, all clocking is disabled (only available function is AHB access).
NOTE: The SARADC trim is also reset and therefore the SAR needs to be re-calibrated, or previously saved trim values re-written if the IP is disabled.
The only functioing system is the AHB register access when the IP is disabled.

1: MCPASS IP enabled. Clocks ungated, Reset negated</description>
                <bitRange>[31:31]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>CMD_RUN</name>
            <description>Run Command register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x4</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x00000001</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>RUN</name>
                <description>When firmware writes a '1' here it will trigger the Autonomous Controller to run, hardware clears this bit when the Autonomous Controller acknowledges the run command.  This command has no action if the Autonomous Controller is already running.
When the AC is stopped, the AC may be set back into running state by setting the CMD STATE to the address ofone ore more instructions and by setting this RUN bit to '1'</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToSet</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>CMD_STATE</name>
            <description>State Override register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x8</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x0000000F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>STATE</name>
                <description>Autonomous Controller CMD_STATE register.  This register indicates which instruction the AC will start execution from when it receives a RUN command.  Software can overrwrite this field only when STATUS.STATUS=STOPPED to set the AC to execute from a specific STATE when triggered by the RUN command.

When first enabled, this register is reset to 0, so by default the AC starts executing at state 0.</description>
                <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>BLOCK_STATUS</name>
            <description>Block Ready status register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x10</addressOffset>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetMask>0x00000001</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>READY</name>
                <description>This bit reflects the AC BLOCK_READY condition, which can be polled by SW to determine if all the enabled blocks have settled and are ready for use</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>STATUS</name>
            <description>AC Status register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x20</addressOffset>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetMask>0x0000030F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>CUR_STATE</name>
                <description>This register indicates where in memory the AC is currently executing instructions.</description>
                <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>STATUS</name>
                <description>This field indicates if the Autonomous Controller is busy</description>
                <bitRange>[9:8]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>STOPPED</name>
                    <description>Autonomous Controller is stopped</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>RUNNING</name>
                    <description>Autonomous Controller is running</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>RSVD2</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>2</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>RSVD3</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>3</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>CFG</name>
            <description>AC Config register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x24</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x0000001F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>DOUT_EN</name>
                <description>DOUT_EN controls the GPIO output drive mode though the HSIOM
This register enables the DOUT feature, where up to 5 GPIOs can be controlled programatically with the AC (ACTRLR.TIMER_TABLE.TT_CFG0.DOUT).
To use this feature:
1) The HSIOM (High Speed I/O Mux) device configuration must be set to select one or more DOUT/DOUT_EN bits to drive the associated GPIOs.
2) The associated bit(s) in this register must be set to enable the GPIO for output drive mode.
0 :DOUT[n] GPIO output disabled
1: DOUT[n] GPIO output enabled</description>
                <bitRange>[4:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <dim>2</dim>
            <dimIncrement>4</dimIncrement>
            <name>CNTR_STATUS[%s]</name>
            <description>Loop/Interval counter status register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x100</addressOffset>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetMask>0x800FFF0F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>CUR_STATE</name>
                <description>This field indicates which Timer Table State the counter is assigned.  Valid numbers are 0-15</description>
                <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CUR_CNT</name>
                <description>This field indicates the count value for the given counter.</description>
                <bitRange>[19:8]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BUSY</name>
                <description>This field indicates if the counter is busy</description>
                <bitRange>[31:31]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <cluster>
            <dim>16</dim>
            <dimIncrement>16</dimIncrement>
            <name>TTCFG[%s]</name>
            <description>Timer Table Structure</description>
            <addressOffset>0x800</addressOffset>
            <register>
              <name>TT_CFG0</name>
              <description>AC Configuration 0</description>
              <addressOffset>0x0</addressOffset>
              <resetMask>0x01F1FF90</resetMask>
              <fields>
                <field>
                  <name>SAR_UNLOCK</name>
                  <description>SAR Unlock
0: Lock: SAR fields: EN does NOT get updated
1: Unlock: SAR fields: EN does get updated

These fields are used to prevent unintentional mode transitions.  For example, when you wish to enable the SAR, you set this SAR_UNLOCK bit to '1' in that state.  As other states will have a '0' for this field, and therefore the SAR_EN will not be inadvertantly be set to '0' in other states.  If you want to disable the SAR, set this SAR_UNLOCK bit to '1' again and the SAR_EN to '0' for the disable to take effect.</description>
                  <bitRange>[4:4]</bitRange>
                </field>
                <field>
                  <name>CSG_UNLOCK</name>
                  <description>CSG Unlock
0: Lock: CSG fields: EN does NOT get updated
1: Unlock: CSG fields: EN does get updated</description>
                  <bitRange>[15:7]</bitRange>
                </field>
                <field>
                  <name>DOUT_UNLOCK</name>
                  <description>DOUT Unlock
0: Lock: DOUT does NOT get updated
1: Unlock: DOUT does get updated</description>
                  <bitRange>[16:16]</bitRange>
                </field>
                <field>
                  <name>DOUT</name>
                  <description>MCPASS data out generation
When this function is enabled (see ACTRLR.CFG.DOUT_EN), any enabled bits in this field will be driven through HSIOM to the associated GPIO pads for output.  This allows the AC to digital control to an off chip  resource (e.g. enable a sensor) as part of AC programming.</description>
                  <bitRange>[24:20]</bitRange>
                </field>
              </fields>
            </register>
            <register>
              <name>TT_CFG1</name>
              <description>AC Configuration 1</description>
              <addressOffset>0x4</addressOffset>
              <resetMask>0x00000000</resetMask>
              <fields>
                <field>
                  <name>BR_ADDR</name>
                  <description>Absolute address of the branch if taken (0-15 possible)</description>
                  <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
                </field>
                <field>
                  <name>NC_7_4</name>
                  <description>N/A</description>
                  <bitRange>[7:4]</bitRange>
                </field>
                <field>
                  <name>COND</name>
                  <description>Autonomous Controller Condition</description>
                  <bitRange>[14:8]</bitRange>
                </field>
                <field>
                  <name>NC_15</name>
                  <description>N/A</description>
                  <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
                </field>
                <field>
                  <name>ACTION</name>
                  <description>Autonomous Controller Action</description>
                  <bitRange>[18:16]</bitRange>
                  <enumeratedValues>
                    <enumeratedValue>
                      <name>STOP</name>
                      <description>Stop the autonomous controller</description>
                      <value>0</value>
                    </enumeratedValue>
                    <enumeratedValue>
                      <name>NEXT</name>
                      <description>Execute the current state and proceed to the next state</description>
                      <value>1</value>
                    </enumeratedValue>
                    <enumeratedValue>
                      <name>WAIT_FOR</name>
                      <description>Wait for the associated COND to occur before moving to the next state

If COND=FALSE, stay in the present state indefinitely.  If COND=TRUE, move to the next state.</description>
                      <value>2</value>
                    </enumeratedValue>
                    <enumeratedValue>
                      <name>BRANCH_IF_TRUE</name>
                      <description>Check if COND has occurred, If it has, take the indicated branch (BR ADDR), if not, proceed to the next state.

If COND=FALSE, proceed to the next state.  If COND=TRUE, take the indicated branch (BR_ADDR).</description>
                      <value>3</value>
                    </enumeratedValue>
                    <enumeratedValue>
                      <name>BRANCH_IF_FALSE</name>
                      <description>Check if COND has occurred, If it has proceed to the next state, if not, take the indicated branch (BR ADDR)

If COND=FALSE,  take the indicated branch (BR_ADDR).  If COND=TRUE, proceed to the next state.</description>
                      <value>4</value>
                    </enumeratedValue>
                    <enumeratedValue>
                      <name>RSVD_0</name>
                      <description>N/A</description>
                      <value>5</value>
                    </enumeratedValue>
                    <enumeratedValue>
                      <name>RSVD_1</name>
                      <description>N/A</description>
                      <value>6</value>
                    </enumeratedValue>
                    <enumeratedValue>
                      <name>RSVD_2</name>
                      <description>N/A</description>
                      <value>7</value>
                    </enumeratedValue>
                  </enumeratedValues>
                </field>
                <field>
                  <name>INTR_SET</name>
                  <description>AC Interrupt Set.  This will generate a CPU interrupt or Hardware Trigger output if configured.  If ACTION=WAIT_FOR, the interrupt is generated after the COND occurs.
The AC Interrupt may be configured in MCPASS_MMIO.MCPASS_INTR.
The AC Hardware trigger may be configured in MCPASS_MMIO.TR_PULSE_OUT</description>
                  <bitRange>[19:19]</bitRange>
                </field>
                <field>
                  <name>CNT</name>
                  <description>This field is used to set the timer/counter values for either interval and loop counts.  The actual count is CNT value + 1.  Therefore, the range is 1 to 4096.  The value of 1 is degenerate.</description>
                  <bitRange>[31:20]</bitRange>
                </field>
              </fields>
            </register>
            <register>
              <name>TT_CFG2</name>
              <description>AC Configuration 2</description>
              <addressOffset>0x8</addressOffset>
              <resetMask>0x00000000</resetMask>
              <fields>
                <field>
                  <name>CSG_EN</name>
                  <description>CSG Slice Enables.  The CSG UNLOCK bits must be set in the same state as these bits (see TT_CFG0) to enable or disable. These bits are normally used in conjunction with ACTION=WAIT_FOR and COND=BLOCK_READY</description>
                  <bitRange>[8:0]</bitRange>
                </field>
                <field>
                  <name>CSG_DAC_TR</name>
                  <description>CSG Slice Triggers.  After the block is ready for use - these bits may be used for CSG DAC START and UPDATE triggers.  The type of trigger is programmed in the CSG_SLICE register block.
 If ACTION=WAIT_FOR, the trigger always occurs in the fir cycle of that state</description>
                  <bitRange>[17:9]</bitRange>
                </field>
                <field>
                  <name>RSVD</name>
                  <description>N/A</description>
                  <bitRange>[31:18]</bitRange>
                </field>
              </fields>
            </register>
            <register>
              <name>TT_CFG3</name>
              <description>AC Configuration 3</description>
              <addressOffset>0xC</addressOffset>
              <resetMask>0x00000000</resetMask>
              <fields>
                <field>
                  <name>SAR_TR</name>
                  <description>SAR Table Entry Triggers.  After the block is ready for use - these bits may be used to trigger one ore more SAR Entries.  If ACTION=WAIT_FOR, the trigger always occurs in the fir cycle of that state</description>
                  <bitRange>[7:0]</bitRange>
                </field>
                <field>
                  <name>SAR_EN</name>
                  <description>SARADC Enable   The SAR UNLOCK bit must be set in the same state as this bit (see TT_CFG0) to enable or disable. This bit is normally used in conjunction with ACTION=WAIT_FOR and COND=BLOCK_READY</description>
                  <bitRange>[8:8]</bitRange>
                </field>
                <field>
                  <name>NC_11_9</name>
                  <description>N/A</description>
                  <bitRange>[11:9]</bitRange>
                </field>
                <field>
                  <name>SAR_AROUTE_TR</name>
                  <description>SAR Aroute Trigger (1 bit per mux) to change Route setting for one or more AROUTE MUXes.  Set this bit in conjuction with a new SAR_AROUTE_SEL value.</description>
                  <bitRange>[15:12]</bitRange>
                </field>
                <field>
                  <name>SAR_AROUTE_SEL</name>
                  <description>SAR Route setting (3 bits per mux) to select the routing input.  The routing bits in this field are only valid if the associated SAR_ROUTE_TR is set.</description>
                  <bitRange>[27:16]</bitRange>
                </field>
                <field>
                  <name>NC_31_28</name>
                  <description>N/A</description>
                  <bitRange>[31:28]</bitRange>
                </field>
              </fields>
            </register>
          </cluster>
        </cluster>
        <cluster>
          <name>INFRA</name>
          <description>MCPASS infrastructure</description>
          <addressOffset>0xE0000</addressOffset>
          <register>
            <name>TR_IN_SEL</name>
            <description>Trigger Input Select Triggers 0-7</description>
            <addressOffset>0x0</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x77777777</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>TR0_SEL</name>
                <description>Trigger selection.
NOTE: ALL TRIGGERS ARE GATED (DISABLED) BY HARDWARE UNTIL BLOCK READY IS '1'</description>
                <bitRange>[2:0]</bitRange>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DISABLED</name>
                    <description>Trigger is off</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>HW_TR_A</name>
                    <description>Select an external HW trigger from A trigger inputs.  For additional options see HW_TR_MODE</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>HW_TR_B</name>
                    <description>Select the external HW trigger from B trigger inputs. For additional options see HW_TR_MODE</description>
                    <value>2</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>FW_PULSE</name>
                    <description>Select FW PULSE trigger.  See FW_TR_IN_PULSE.</description>
                    <value>3</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>FW_LEVEL</name>
                    <description>Select FW LEVEL trigger.  See FW_TR_IN_LEVEL.</description>
                    <value>4</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TR1_SEL</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[6:4]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TR2_SEL</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[10:8]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TR3_SEL</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[14:12]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TR4_SEL</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[18:16]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TR5_SEL</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[22:20]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TR6_SEL</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[26:24]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TR7_SEL</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[30:28]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>TR_IN_SEL_2</name>
            <description>Trigger Input Select Triggers 8-15</description>
            <addressOffset>0x4</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x77777777</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>TR8_SEL</name>
                <description>Trigger selection.
NOTE: ALL TRIGGERS ARE GATED (DISABLED) BY HARDWARE UNTIL BLOCK READY IS '1'</description>
                <bitRange>[2:0]</bitRange>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DISABLED</name>
                    <description>Trigger is off</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>HW_TR_A</name>
                    <description>Select an external HW trigger from A trigger inputs.  For additional options see HW_TR_MODE</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>HW_TR_B</name>
                    <description>Select the external HW trigger from B trigger inputs. For additional options see HW_TR_MODE</description>
                    <value>2</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>FW_PULSE</name>
                    <description>Select FW PULSE trigger.  See FW_TR_IN_PULSE.</description>
                    <value>3</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>FW_LEVEL</name>
                    <description>Select FW LEVEL trigger.  See FW_TR_IN_LEVEL.</description>
                    <value>4</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TR9_SEL</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[6:4]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TR10_SEL</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[10:8]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TR11_SEL</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[14:12]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TR12_SEL</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[18:16]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TR13_SEL</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[22:20]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TR14_SEL</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[26:24]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TR15_SEL</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[30:28]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>HW_TR_MODE</name>
            <description>HW Trigger Mode Triggers 0-7</description>
            <addressOffset>0x8</addressOffset>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>HW_TR0_MODE</name>
                <description>If HW trigger is selected, this controls the trigger mode.</description>
                <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>PULSE_ON_POS_DOUBLE_SYNC</name>
                    <description>HW trigger is assumed to be async in FC, it is double synced and converted to pulse on positive edge.</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>PULSE_ON_NEG_DOUBLE_SYNC</name>
                    <description>HW trigger is assumed to be async in FC, it is double synced and converted to synchronous pulse on negative edge.</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>PULSE_ON_BOTH_DOUBLE_SYNC</name>
                    <description>HW trigger is assumed to be async in FC, it is double synced and converted to synchronous pulse on both edges.</description>
                    <value>2</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>LEVEL_DOUBLE_SYNC</name>
                    <description>HW trigger is assumed to be async in FC, it is double synced and used as a level trigger.
NOTE: Level trigger selection is only for use by a CSG Slice in Hyteretic Mode and the minimum duration of the high or low level must be greater than or equal to the CSG clock period.</description>
                    <value>3</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>PULSE_ON_POS_SINGLE_SYNC</name>
                    <description>HW trigger is assumed to sync in FC, captured in a register, and then converted to pulse on positive edge</description>
                    <value>4</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>PULSE_ON_NEG_SINGLE_SYNC</name>
                    <description>HW trigger is assumed to sync in FC, captured in a register, and then converted to pulse on negative edge</description>
                    <value>5</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>PULSE_ON_BOTH_SINGLE_SYNC</name>
                    <description>HW trigger is assumed to sync in FC, captured in a register, and then converted to pulse on both edges</description>
                    <value>6</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>LEVEL_SINGLE_SYNC</name>
                    <description>HW trigger is assumed to be async in FC, captured in a register, and used as a level trigger.
NOTE: Level trigger selection is only for use by a CSG Slice in Hyteretic Mode and the minimum duration of the high or low level must be greater than or equal to the CSG clock period.</description>
                    <value>7</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>HW_TR1_MODE</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[7:4]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>HW_TR2_MODE</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[11:8]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>HW_TR3_MODE</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[15:12]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>HW_TR4_MODE</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[19:16]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>HW_TR5_MODE</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[23:20]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>HW_TR6_MODE</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[27:24]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>HW_TR7_MODE</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[31:28]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>HW_TR_MODE_2</name>
            <description>HW Trigger Mode Triggers 8-15</description>
            <addressOffset>0xC</addressOffset>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>HW_TR8_MODE</name>
                <description>If HW trigger is selected, this controls the trigger mode.</description>
                <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>PULSE_ON_POS_DOUBLE_SYNC</name>
                    <description>HW trigger is assumed to be async in FC, it is double synced and converted to pulse on positive edge.</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>PULSE_ON_NEG_DOUBLE_SYNC</name>
                    <description>HW trigger is assumed to be async in FC, it is double synced and converted to synchronous pulse on negative edge.</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>PULSE_ON_BOTH_DOUBLE_SYNC</name>
                    <description>HW trigger is assumed to be async in FC, it is double synced and converted to synchronous pulse on both edges.</description>
                    <value>2</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>LEVEL_DOUBLE_SYNC</name>
                    <description>HW trigger is assumed to be async in FC, it is double synced and used as a level trigger.
NOTE: Level trigger selection is only for use by a CSG Slice in Hyteretic Mode and the minimum duration of the high or low level must be greater than or equal to the CSG clock period.</description>
                    <value>3</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>PULSE_ON_POS_SINGLE_SYNC</name>
                    <description>HW trigger is assumed to sync in FC, captured in a register, and then converted to pulse on positive edge</description>
                    <value>4</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>PULSE_ON_NEG_SINGLE_SYNC</name>
                    <description>HW trigger is assumed to sync in FC, captured in a register, and then converted to pulse on negative edge</description>
                    <value>5</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>PULSE_ON_BOTH_SINGLE_SYNC</name>
                    <description>HW trigger is assumed to sync in FC, captured in a register, and then converted to pulse on both edges</description>
                    <value>6</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>LEVEL_SINGLE_SYNC</name>
                    <description>HW trigger is assumed to be async in FC, captured in a register, and used as a level trigger.
NOTE: Level trigger selection is only for use by a CSG Slice in Hyteretic Mode and the minimum duration of the high or low level must be greater than or equal to the CSG clock period.</description>
                    <value>7</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>HW_TR9_MODE</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[7:4]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>HW_TR10_MODE</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[11:8]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>HW_TR11_MODE</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[15:12]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>HW_TR12_MODE</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[19:16]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>HW_TR13_MODE</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[23:20]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>HW_TR14_MODE</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[27:24]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>HW_TR15_MODE</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[31:28]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>FW_TR_PULSE</name>
            <description>FW Pulse Mode Trigger</description>
            <addressOffset>0x10</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x0000FFFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>FW_TR_PULSE</name>
                <description>If FW is selected in TR_IN_SEL, A write of '1' to this register will generate an internal PULSE trigger. These bits will be cleared automatically by HW.

Firmware Rule:
Poll on ACTRLR.BLOCK_STATUS to become '1' before issuing firmware triggers</description>
                <bitRange>[15:0]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToSet</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>FW_TR_LEVEL</name>
            <description>FW Level Mode Trigger</description>
            <addressOffset>0x14</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x0000FFFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>FW_TR_LEVEL</name>
                <description>If FW is selected in TR_IN_SEL, Writes to this register will be passed to the internal trigger as a LEVEL '1' or '0.
NOTE: LEVEL mode trigger is only valid for CSG module,  Do not use for SAR Entry.

Firmware Rule:
Poll on ACTRLR.BLOCK_STATUS to become '1' before issuing firmware triggers</description>
                <bitRange>[15:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>CLOCK_STARTUP_DIV</name>
            <description>Startup Clock Divder</description>
            <addressOffset>0x20</addressOffset>
            <resetValue>0x00000007</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x000000FF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>DIV_VAL</name>
                <description>Divide MCPASS a prescaled clock divide to generate block startup delays.  Actual divide value is DIV_VAL+1.
Typically this register should be set to give a period of 200ns, so the count depends on CLK_HF.
NOTE: This counter is clocked on CLK_HF/2
Examples:
For CLK_HF = 80MHz (CLK_INFRA = 40MHz), set DIV_VAL to 7 (8 cycles * 25ns = 200ns, default)
For CLK_HF = 160MHz (CLK_INFRA = 80MHz) set DIV_VAL to 15 (16 cycles * 12.5ns = 200ns)
For CLK_HF = 240MHz (CLK_INFRA = 120MHz) set DIV_VAL to 23 (24 cycles * 8.33ns = 200ns</description>
                <bitRange>[7:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <dim>4</dim>
            <dimIncrement>4</dimIncrement>
            <name>STARTUP_CFG[%s]</name>
            <description>Startup configuration</description>
            <addressOffset>0x30</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x00000FFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>COUNT_VAL</name>
                <description>Actual count value is COUNT_VAL+1</description>
                <bitRange>[7:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SAR_EN</name>
                <description>SAR startup</description>
                <bitRange>[8:8]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CSG_CH_EN</name>
                <description>CSG Channel Enable startup</description>
                <bitRange>[9:9]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CSG_PWR_EN_SLICE</name>
                <description>CSG Slice Enable startup</description>
                <bitRange>[10:10]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CSG_READY</name>
                <description>CSG Auto Zero/Comparator gate startup
When this bit is set, the associated COUNT_VAL must be calculated relative to the CLK_CSG divide and the resulting period must be 33 CLK_CSG periods
Examples:
Assuming we have 200ns prescale value programmed into CLOCK_STARTUP_DIV.DIV_VAL.
With the examples of CLK_CSG as configured by MXPERI at the following frequencies:
For CSG at 40 MHz, 25ns x 33 cycles = 825ns.  roundup(825/200) = 5 (COUNT_VAL = 4)
For CSG at 80 Mhz,  12.5ns x 33 cycles = 412ns  roundup(412/200) = 3 (COUNT_VAL = 2)
For CSG at 120 MHz, 8.33 ns x 33 cycles = 275ns roundup(275/200) = 2 (COUNT_VAL = 1)</description>
                <bitRange>[11:11]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>VDDA_STATUS</name>
            <description>Analog Voltage Status</description>
            <addressOffset>0x50</addressOffset>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetMask>0x00000001</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>VDDA_OK</name>
                <description>Read only status bit to determine the presence of VDDA.  This bit is set by HW and it must be 1 prior to enabling the SAR or CSG.
0: VDDA is below minimum threshold
1: VDDA is at or above minimum threhold</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>ANA_CTRL</name>
            <description>Analog Control</description>
            <addressOffset>0x70</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x0000000F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>FORCE_POR_0</name>
                <description>Override for Analog POR Hardware circuit.
0: (default) POR is controlled by analog hardware
1: Force POR asserted (POR is an active low signal)
If the MCPASS is not in use, set this bit to '1' to achieve the lowest level of IP leakage
NOTE: If this bit is set to 1, FORCE_POR_1 must be 0.</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>FORCE_POR_1</name>
                <description>Override for Analog POR Hardware circuit.
0: (default) POR is controlled by analog hardware
1: Force POR negated (POR is an active low signal)
NOTE: If this bit is set to 1, FORCE_POR_0 must be 0.</description>
                <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>FORCE_OVERLOAD_EN_0</name>
                <description>Override for Analog OVERLOAD Hardware circuit.
0: (default) OVERLOAD enable is controlled by ACTRL.CTRL.ENABLED bit
1: Force OVERLOAD enable off
NOTE: If this bit is set to 1, FORCE_OVERLOAD_EN_1 must be 0</description>
                <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>FORCE_OVERLOAD_EN_1</name>
                <description>Override for Analog OVERLOAD Hardware circuit
0: (default) OVERLOAD enable is controlled by ACTRL.CTRL.ENABLED bit
1: Force OVERLOAD enable on
NOTE:  If this bit is set to 1, FORCE_OVERLOAD_EN_0 must be 0</description>
                <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>GPIO_ISO_CTRL</name>
            <description>GPIO Isolation Controls</description>
            <addressOffset>0x100</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x0000FFFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>GPIO_FORCE_ISOLATION_ON</name>
                <description>Force the Isolation signal to 'Connected' for GPIO. A value of 1 indicates the isolation switch is closed and the output is connected to the input.</description>
                <bitRange>[7:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>GPIO_FORCE_ISOLATION_OFF</name>
                <description>Force the Isolation signal OFF for GPIO. A value of 1 indicates the isolation switch is open and the output is NOT connected to the input.</description>
                <bitRange>[15:8]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <cluster>
            <name>AREFV2</name>
            <description>AREF Configuration</description>
            <addressOffset>0xE00</addressOffset>
            <register>
              <name>AREF_CTRL</name>
              <description>AREF control</description>
              <addressOffset>0x0</addressOffset>
              <resetMask>0x8031FFFD</resetMask>
              <fields>
                <field>
                  <name>AREF_MODE</name>
                  <description>N/A</description>
                  <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                </field>
                <field>
                  <name>AREF_BIAS_SCALE</name>
                  <description>N/A</description>
                  <bitRange>[3:2]</bitRange>
                </field>
                <field>
                  <name>AREF_RMB</name>
                  <description>N/A</description>
                  <bitRange>[6:4]</bitRange>
                </field>
                <field>
                  <name>CTB_IPTAT_SCALE</name>
                  <description>N/A</description>
                  <bitRange>[7:7]</bitRange>
                </field>
                <field>
                  <name>CTB_IPTAT_REDIRECT</name>
                  <description>N/A</description>
                  <bitRange>[15:8]</bitRange>
                </field>
                <field>
                  <name>IZTAT_SEL</name>
                  <description>N/A</description>
                  <bitRange>[16:16]</bitRange>
                  <enumeratedValues>
                    <enumeratedValue>
                      <name>RSVD</name>
                      <description>N/A</description>
                      <value>0</value>
                    </enumeratedValue>
                    <enumeratedValue>
                      <name>LOCAL</name>
                      <description>Use locally generated 250nA</description>
                      <value>1</value>
                    </enumeratedValue>
                  </enumeratedValues>
                </field>
                <field>
                  <name>VREF_SEL</name>
                  <description>N/A</description>
                  <bitRange>[21:20]</bitRange>
                  <enumeratedValues>
                    <enumeratedValue>
                      <name>RSVD</name>
                      <description>N/A</description>
                      <value>0</value>
                    </enumeratedValue>
                    <enumeratedValue>
                      <name>LOCAL</name>
                      <description>Use locally generated Vref</description>
                      <value>1</value>
                    </enumeratedValue>
                    <enumeratedValue>
                      <name>EXTERNAL</name>
                      <description>Use externally supplied Vref (aref_ext_vref)</description>
                      <value>2</value>
                    </enumeratedValue>
                  </enumeratedValues>
                </field>
                <field>
                  <name>ENABLED</name>
                  <description>- 0: AREF disabled
- 1: AREF enabled</description>
                  <bitRange>[31:31]</bitRange>
                </field>
              </fields>
            </register>
          </cluster>
          <register>
            <name>VREF_TRIM0</name>
            <description>VREF Trim bits</description>
            <addressOffset>0xF00</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x000000FF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>VREF_ABS_TRIM</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[7:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>VREF_TRIM1</name>
            <description>VREF Trim bits</description>
            <addressOffset>0xF04</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x000000FF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>VREF_TEMPCO_TRIM</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[7:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>VREF_TRIM2</name>
            <description>VREF Trim bits</description>
            <addressOffset>0xF08</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x000000FF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>VREF_CURV_TRIM</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[7:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>VREF_TRIM3</name>
            <description>VREF Trim bits</description>
            <addressOffset>0xF0C</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x0000000F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>VREF_ATTEN_TRIM</name>
                <description>Obsolete</description>
                <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>IZTAT_TRIM0</name>
            <description>IZTAT Trim bits</description>
            <addressOffset>0xF10</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x000000FF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>IZTAT_ABS_TRIM</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[7:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>IZTAT_TRIM1</name>
            <description>IZTAT Trim bits</description>
            <addressOffset>0xF14</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x000000FF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>IZTAT_TC_TRIM</name>
                <description>IZTAT temperature correction trim (RMB)
0x00 : No IZTAT temperature correction
0xFF : Maximum IZTAT temperature correction

As this is a Risk Mitigation Register, it should be loaded with 0x08.</description>
                <bitRange>[7:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>IPTAT_TRIM0</name>
            <description>IPTAT Trim bits</description>
            <addressOffset>0xF18</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x000000FF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>IPTAT_CORE_TRIM</name>
                <description>IPTAT trim
0x0 : Minimum IPTAT current (~150nA at room)
0xF : Maximum IPTAT current (~350nA at room)</description>
                <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>IPTAT_CTBM_TRIM</name>
                <description>CTMB PTAT Current Trim
0x0 : Minimum CTMB IPTAT Current (~875nA)
0xF : Maximum CTMB IPTAT Current (~1.1uA)</description>
                <bitRange>[7:4]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>ICTAT_TRIM0</name>
            <description>ICTAT Trim bits</description>
            <addressOffset>0xF1C</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x0000000F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>ICTAT_TRIM</name>
                <description>ICTAT trim
0x00 : Minimum ICTAT current (~150nA at room)
0x0F : Maximum ICTAT current (~350nA at room)</description>
                <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>ANA_COMMON_CTRL</name>
            <description>AFE Common Control</description>
            <addressOffset>0xF20</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x0000000F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>AFE_VCM_TEST</name>
                <description>AFE VCM Test mode control
0: AFE VCM voltage is not sent  to test bus
1:AFE VCM voltage is sent to test bus</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>ADC_AGND_CTRL</name>
                <description>0x0 - AGND, 0x1 uses AN_A4 as ADC ground</description>
                <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>AFE_VCM_CTRL</name>
                <description>Bit 0 controls vddq switch and bit 1 controls varef switch
&lt;00&gt; - VCM gen block off, outputs vssa
&lt;01&gt; - VCM gen block connects to vdda and outputs vdda/2
&lt;10&gt; - VCM gen block connects to varef_ext and outputs varef_ext/2</description>
                <bitRange>[3:2]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
        </cluster>
        <cluster>
          <name>MMIO</name>
          <description>MCPASS top-level MMIO</description>
          <addressOffset>0xF0000</addressOffset>
          <register>
            <name>FIFO_INTR</name>
            <description>FIFO Interrupt request register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x0</addressOffset>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetMask>0x0000000F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>FIFO_LEVEL</name>
                <description>This field will be '1', when STATUS.USED &gt;= CTRL.LEVEL+1 and '0' when STATUS.USED &lt; CTRL.LEVEL+1 for the indicated FIFO. This interrupt is level based.</description>
                <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>FIFO_INTR_SET</name>
            <description>FIFO Interrupt set request register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x10</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x0000000F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>FIFO_LEVEL_SET</name>
                <description>Write with '1' to set corresponding bit in interrupt request register.
Write with '0' to clear corresponding bit in interrupt request register.
NOTE: This register has different functionality than normal event based interrupt register as this interrupt is LEVEL based</description>
                <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>FIFO_INTR_MASK</name>
            <description>FIFO Interrupt mask register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x20</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x0000000F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>FIFO_LEVEL_MASK</name>
                <description>Mask bit for corresponding bit in interrupt request register.</description>
                <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>FIFO_INTR_MASKED</name>
            <description>FIFO Interrupt masked request register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x30</addressOffset>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetMask>0x0000000F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>FIFO_LEVEL_MASKED</name>
                <description>Logical and of corresponding request and mask bits.</description>
                <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>HPPASS_INTR</name>
            <description>MCPASS Interrupt request register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x40</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x000017FF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>FIFO_OVERFLOW</name>
                <description>HW sets this field to '1', when writing to a full FIFO. See FIFO_CFG/STATUS (read and write pointers) and FIFO_CFG/USED register for detailed infomration.
Write with '1' to clear bit.</description>
                <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>FIFO_UNDERFLOW</name>
                <description>HW sets this field to '1', when reading from an empty FIFO.. See FIFO_CFG/STATUS (read and write pointers) and FIFO_CFG/USED register for detailed infomration.
Write with '1' to clear bit.</description>
                <bitRange>[7:4]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RESULT_OVERFLOW</name>
                <description>HW sets this field to '1 on RESULT overflow.  This bit is set when new SAR Entry or FIR results are written and the associated bit the the INTR_SAR_RESULT register bit is set, implying the previous results have not been read.
To determine which Entry or FIR results caused this see SAR_CFG/RESULT_OVERFLOW.
Write a '1' to clear this bit.</description>
                <bitRange>[8:8]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>ENTRY_TR_COLLISION</name>
                <description>HW sets this bit if a SAR Entry trigger is asserted while that Entry is in progress, from triggering until conversion is complete.
Write a '1' to clear this bit.</description>
                <bitRange>[9:9]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>ENTRY_HOLD_VIOLATION</name>
                <description>HW sets this bit if the samplers in a SAR Entry are in HOLD state longer than the programmed count value .
Write a '1' to clear this bit.</description>
                <bitRange>[10:10]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>AC_INT</name>
                <description>HW sets this field to '1', when the AC generates a programmed interrupt.
Write with '1' to clear bit.</description>
                <bitRange>[12:12]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>HPPASS_INTR_SET</name>
            <description>MCPASS Interrupt set request register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x44</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x000017FF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>FIFO_OVERFLOW_SET</name>
                <description>Write with '1' to set corresponding bit in interrupt request register. (a write of '0' has no effect).</description>
                <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToSet</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>FIFO_UNDERFLOW_SET</name>
                <description>Write with '1' to set corresponding bit in interrupt request register. (a write of '0' has no effect).</description>
                <bitRange>[7:4]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToSet</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RESULT_OVERFLOW_SET</name>
                <description>Write with '1' to set corresponding bit in interrupt request register. (a write of '0' has no effect).</description>
                <bitRange>[8:8]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToSet</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>ENTRY_TR_COLLISION_SET</name>
                <description>Write with '1' to set corresponding bit in interrupt request register. (a write of '0' has no effect).</description>
                <bitRange>[9:9]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToSet</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>ENTRY_HOLD_VIOLATION_SET</name>
                <description>Write with '1' to set corresponding bit in interrupt request register. (a write of '0' has no effect).</description>
                <bitRange>[10:10]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToSet</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>AC_INT_SET</name>
                <description>Write with '1' to set corresponding bit in interrupt request register. (a write of '0' has no effect).</description>
                <bitRange>[12:12]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToSet</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>HPPASS_INTR_MASK</name>
            <description>MCPASS Interrupt mask register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x48</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x000017FF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>FIFO_OVERFLOW_MASK</name>
                <description>Mask bit for corresponding bit in interrupt request register.</description>
                <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>FIFO_UNDERFLOW_MASK</name>
                <description>Mask bit for corresponding bit in interrupt request register.</description>
                <bitRange>[7:4]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RESULT_OVERFLOW_MASK</name>
                <description>Mask bit for corresponding bit in interrupt request register.</description>
                <bitRange>[8:8]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>ENTRY_TR_COLLISION_MASK</name>
                <description>Mask bit for corresponding bit in interrupt request register.</description>
                <bitRange>[9:9]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>ENTRY_HOLD_VIOLATION_MASK</name>
                <description>Mask bit for corresponding bit in interrupt request register.</description>
                <bitRange>[10:10]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>AC_INT_MASK</name>
                <description>Mask bit for corresponding bit in interrupt request register.</description>
                <bitRange>[12:12]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>HPPASS_INTR_MASKED</name>
            <description>MCPASS Interrupt masked request register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x4C</addressOffset>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetMask>0x000017FF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>FIFO_OVERFLOW_MASKED</name>
                <description>Logical and of corresponding request and mask bits.</description>
                <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>FIFO_UNDERFLOW_MASKED</name>
                <description>Logical and of corresponding request and mask bits.</description>
                <bitRange>[7:4]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RESULT_OVERFLOW_MASKED</name>
                <description>Logical and of corresponding request and mask bits.</description>
                <bitRange>[8:8]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>ENTRY_TR_COLLISION_MASKED</name>
                <description>Logical and of corresponding request and mask bits.</description>
                <bitRange>[9:9]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>ENTRY_HOLD_VIOLATION_MASKED</name>
                <description>Logical and of corresponding request and mask bits.</description>
                <bitRange>[10:10]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>AC_INT_MASKED</name>
                <description>Logical and of corresponding request and mask bits.</description>
                <bitRange>[12:12]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>TR_LEVEL_CFG</name>
            <description>LEVEL Trigger out configuration</description>
            <addressOffset>0x50</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x0000FFFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>BYPASS_SYNC</name>
                <description>When this bit is set, the output flip-flop for the given TR_LEVEL_OUT is bypassed.
This option may only be used if the trigger is considered asynchronous and the destination has double sync input.</description>
                <bitRange>[15:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <dim>16</dim>
            <dimIncrement>4</dimIncrement>
            <name>TR_LEVEL_OUT[%s]</name>
            <description>LEVEL Trigger out select register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x60</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0xFF003FFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>CMP_TR</name>
                <description>Enable the selected CMP outputs which are then ORed and routed to the associated trigger</description>
                <bitRange>[8:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CMP_LOGIC_REMAP_TR</name>
                <description>Enable the selected CMP Logic Remap output which are then ORed and routed to the associated trigger</description>
                <bitRange>[11:9]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>DAC_FIFO_BELOW_THRESHOLD</name>
                <description>Enable the selected DAC Fifo Below threshold of 3 to trigger a DMA transfer.</description>
                <bitRange>[13:12]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SAR_RANGE_TR</name>
                <description>Enable the selected SAR_RANGE output which are then ORed and routed to the associated trigger</description>
                <bitRange>[31:24]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <dim>16</dim>
            <dimIncrement>4</dimIncrement>
            <name>TR_PULSE_OUT[%s]</name>
            <description>PULSE Trigger out select register</description>
            <addressOffset>0xA0</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x0000000F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>SEL</name>
                <description>Enable one of the available pulse trigger outputs for the associated trigger</description>
                <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DISABLED</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>SAR_ENTRY0_DONE</name>
                    <description>Entry results are ready for reading in the CHAN_RESULT registers</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>SAR_ENTRY1_DONE</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>2</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>SAR_ENTRY2_DONE</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>3</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>SAR_ENTRY3_DONE</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>4</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>SAR_ENTRY4_DONE</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>5</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>SAR_ENTRY5_DONE</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>6</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>SAR_ENTRY6_DONE</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>7</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>SAR_ENTRY7_DONE</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>8</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>SAR_FIR0_DONE</name>
                    <description>FIR results are ready for reading in the FIR_RESULT registers</description>
                    <value>9</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>SAR_FIR1_DONE</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>10</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>AC_TR</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>11</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>HWFILT3P3Z0_STROBE</name>
                    <description>3P3Z Results are ready for reading in the 3P3Z_RESULT registers</description>
                    <value>12</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>HWFILT3P3Z1_STROBE</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>13</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>HWFILT3P3Z2_STROBE</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>14</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <cluster>
            <name>FIFO</name>
            <description>FIFO Configuration</description>
            <addressOffset>0x100</addressOffset>
            <register>
              <name>CFG</name>
              <description>FIFO configuration register.</description>
              <addressOffset>0x0</addressOffset>
              <resetMask>0x000000F3</resetMask>
              <fields>
                <field>
                  <name>SEL</name>
                  <description>N/A</description>
                  <bitRange>[1:0]</bitRange>
                </field>
                <field>
                  <name>FIFO_TR_EN</name>
                  <description>Enable associated FIFO trigger</description>
                  <bitRange>[7:4]</bitRange>
                </field>
              </fields>
            </register>
            <register>
              <name>CLR</name>
              <description>FIFO FW clear register</description>
              <addressOffset>0x4</addressOffset>
              <resetMask>0x0000000F</resetMask>
              <fields>
                <field>
                  <name>CLR</name>
                  <description>Write a '1' to clear status for associated FIFO.  This doesn't actually clear the data, it just resets the pointers.
HW write a '1' to clear the bit when the reset is completed.</description>
                  <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
                  <modifiedWriteValues>oneToSet</modifiedWriteValues>
                </field>
              </fields>
            </register>
            <register>
              <dim>4</dim>
              <dimIncrement>4</dimIncrement>
              <name>LEVEL[%s]</name>
              <description>FIFO level register.</description>
              <addressOffset>0x10</addressOffset>
              <resetMask>0x0000001F</resetMask>
              <fields>
                <field>
                  <name>LEVEL</name>
                  <description>FIFO level set.  A trigger (and optional interrupt) event occurs when USED.USED = LEVEL+1.</description>
                  <bitRange>[4:0]</bitRange>
                </field>
              </fields>
            </register>
            <register>
              <dim>4</dim>
              <dimIncrement>4</dimIncrement>
              <name>RD_DATA[%s]</name>
              <description>FIFO 32-bit read data register</description>
              <addressOffset>0x30</addressOffset>
              <access>read-only</access>
              <resetMask>0x00000000</resetMask>
              <fields>
                <field>
                  <name>RESULT_DATA</name>
                  <description>16-bit SAR/FIR result (lower bits). They are aligned as defined by SAR_CFG.CHAN_CFG.RIGHT_LEFT_ALIGN.
See SAR_CFG.CHAN_CFG.FIFO_SEL and SAR_CFG.FIR_CFG.FIFO_SEL registers to select a target FIFO.
See SAR_CFG.CTRL.CHAN_ID_EN to optionally include a channel ID, otherwise these bits are 0</description>
                  <bitRange>[15:0]</bitRange>
                  <access>read-only</access>
                </field>
                <field>
                  <name>RESULT_CHAN_ID</name>
                  <description>6-bit Channel ID (upper bits),
See SAR_CFG.CHAN_CFG.FIFO_SEL and SAR_CFG.FIR_CFG.FIFO_SEL registers to select a target FIFO.
See SAR_CFG.CTRL.CHAN_ID_EN to optionally include a channel ID, otherwise these bits are 0</description>
                  <bitRange>[21:16]</bitRange>
                  <access>read-only</access>
                </field>
              </fields>
            </register>
            <register>
              <dim>4</dim>
              <dimIncrement>4</dimIncrement>
              <name>USED[%s]</name>
              <description>FIFO used register</description>
              <addressOffset>0x50</addressOffset>
              <access>read-only</access>
              <resetMask>0x0000003F</resetMask>
              <fields>
                <field>
                  <name>USED</name>
                  <description>Number of used/occupied entries in the FIFO.</description>
                  <bitRange>[5:0]</bitRange>
                  <access>read-only</access>
                </field>
              </fields>
            </register>
            <register>
              <dim>4</dim>
              <dimIncrement>4</dimIncrement>
              <name>STATUS[%s]</name>
              <description>FIFO status register</description>
              <addressOffset>0x70</addressOffset>
              <access>read-only</access>
              <resetMask>0x001F001F</resetMask>
              <fields>
                <field>
                  <name>RD_PTR</name>
                  <description>FIFO read pointer: FIFO location from which a data is read.

Note: This functionality is intended for debugging purposes.</description>
                  <bitRange>[4:0]</bitRange>
                  <access>read-only</access>
                </field>
                <field>
                  <name>WR_PTR</name>
                  <description>FIFO write pointer: FIFO location at which a new data is written by the hardware.

Note: This functionality is intended for debugging purposes.</description>
                  <bitRange>[20:16]</bitRange>
                  <access>read-only</access>
                </field>
              </fields>
            </register>
          </cluster>
        </cluster>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>I3C0</name>
      <version>1.0</version>
      <description>I3C controller top</description>
      <headerStructName>I3C</headerStructName>
      <baseAddress>0x42C10000</baseAddress>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0</offset>
        <size>65536</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
        <cluster>
          <name>CORE</name>
          <description>registers in I3C IP core</description>
          <addressOffset>0x0</addressOffset>
          <register>
            <name>DEVICE_CTRL</name>
            <description>Device Control Register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x0</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0xEF000181</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>IBA_INCLUDE</name>
                <description>I3C Broadcast Address Include.

This bit is only valid when this I3C Device is functioning as the Active Controller.

This bit is used to include the I3C broadcast address (0x7E) in private transfers.
Note: If the I3C broadcast address is not included in private transfers, In-band Interrupts (IBI) driven from Targets might not win address arbitration and IBIs will get delayed.

Values:
    0x0: I3C Broadcast Address is not included in Private Transfers
    0x1: I3C Broadcast Address is included in Private Transfers</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>I2C_TARGET_PRESENT</name>
                <description>&lt;HIDDEN&gt;</description>
                <bitRange>[7:7]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>HOT_JOIN_CTRL</name>
                <description>Hot-Join ACK/NACK Control

This bit is only valid when this I3C Device is functioning as the Active Controller.

This bit acts as a global control to ACK/NACK the Hot-Join request from the devices.
The Active Controller ACK/NACKs the Hot-Join request from other devices connected on the I3C Bus, based on programming of this bit.

Values:
    0x0: ACK the Hot-Join request.
    0x1: NACK and send broadcast CCC(auto-disable) to disable Hot-join.</description>
                <bitRange>[8:8]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>IDLE_CNT_MULTPLIER</name>
                <description>Idle count multiplier.

This bit is only valid when this I3C Device is functioning as a Target.

After power-on reset, this Target is enabled only after it sees both SDA and SCL lines idle for a specified time.
This idle time is calculated by multipliying IDLE_CNT_MULTPLIER with BUS_AVAILABLE_TIME field in the BUS_FREE_AVAIL_TIMING register.

Values:
    0x00: BUS_AVAILABLE_TIME * 1
    0x01: BUS_AVAILABLE_TIME * 2
    0x10: BUS_AVAILABLE_TIME * 4
    0x11: BUS_AVAILABLE_TIME * 8</description>
                <bitRange>[25:24]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TARGET_PEC_EN</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[26:26]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>ADAPTIVE_I2C_I3C</name>
                <description>Hot-Join Control for Adaptive I2C/I3C Mode

This bit is only valid when this I3C Device is functioning as a Target.

In Adaptive Mode, this bit is used to enable some features of this target device. This bit is cleared automatically if the controller determines the mode as I3C.

Values:
    0x0: The device initiates a Hot-Join without determining the bus mode and assuming itself to be on I3C bus.
    0x1: The device initiates a Hot-Join request only after it has switched to I3C mode of operation.</description>
                <bitRange>[27:27]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>ABORT</name>
                <description>I3C Abort.

This bit is only valid when this I3C Device is functioning as the Active Controller.

This bit allows this device to relinquish the I3C Bus before completing the issued transfer.
In response to an ABORT request, the Controller issues the STOP condition after the complete data byte is transferred or received.
This bit is auto-cleared once the transfer is aborted and the controller issues a 'Transfer Abort' interrupt.</description>
                <bitRange>[29:29]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RESUME</name>
                <description>I3C Resume

This bit is used to resume the I3C block after it goes to the Halt state.

When functioning as the Active Controller, the block goes to the halt state (as indicated in PRESENT_STATE Register) due to any type of error in the transfer (the type of error is indicated by ERR_STATUS field in the RESPONSE_QUEUE_PORT register).
After the block goes to the Halt state, the application has to write 1'b1 to this bit to resume. This bit is auto-cleared once the block resumes the transfers by initiating the next command.

When functioning as a Target, the block goes to the halt state due to following conditions:
1 =&gt; Any type of error in the transfer (the type of error is indicated by ERR_STATUS field in the RESPONSE_QUEUE_PORT register)
2 =&gt; MRL Register updated by the Active Controller through SETMRL CCC.
After the device has gone to halt state, the application has to take necessary action to handle the error condition and then write 1'b1 to this bit to resume the controller. This bit is auto-cleared once the controller is ready to accept new transfers.</description>
                <bitRange>[30:30]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>ENABLE</name>
                <description>I3C Enable

Controls whether or not I3C functionality is enabled.

When functioning as the Active Controller, software can write to this register to request to disable the I3C block.
However, the block may not get disabled immediately and is 'Disabled' after commands in the Command queue (if any) are executed leading to a STOP condition on the bus and Controller FSM is in IDLE state (as indicated by PRESENT_STATE Register).

When functioning as a Target, software can write to this register to request to disable the I3C block.
However, the disable happens after the ongoing transfer is completed on the I3C bus. Software can read back 1'b0 from this field once disabling of I3C is completed. After power on reset, the software can enable I3C Secondary Controller or Target functionality by programming this bit to 1'b1.
However the I3C bus interface of the block will respond to transfer on the bus, only after it observes Bus Available condition for BUS_AVAILABLE_TIME*IDLE_CNT_MULTPLIER counts of pclk period.
The successful completion of Enable/Disable of the block depends on availability of SCL to the controller at the time of performing this operation, and hence may not happen instantly.

Values:
    0x0: Disables the I3C controller.
    0x1: Enables the I3C controller.</description>
                <bitRange>[31:31]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DEVICE_ADDR</name>
            <description>Device Address Register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x4</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x807F807F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>STATIC_ADDR</name>
                <description>Device Static Address.

This field is only valid when this I3C Device is functioning as a Target.

The device uses this address to respond to SETDASA CCC Command to get the Dynamic Address if static address is valid (STATIC_ADDRESS_VALID must be set to 1).</description>
                <bitRange>[6:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>STATIC_ADDR_VALID</name>
                <description>Static Address Valid.

This field is only valid when this I3C Device is functioning as a Target.

Values:
    0x0: Static Address in STATIC_ADDR field is invalid
    0x1: Static Address in STATIC_ADDR field is valid</description>
                <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>DYNAMIC_ADDR</name>
                <description>Device Dynamic Address.

This field is used to program the Device Dynamic Address. The block uses this address for I3C transfers.
=&gt; When functioning as the Primary Controller, the user/application must program the Dynamic Address.
=&gt; When functioning as a Target, the Active Controller assigns this address during ENTDAA or SETDASA procedures.</description>
                <bitRange>[22:16]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>DYNAMIC_ADDR_VALID</name>
                <description>Dynamic Address Valid

This bit is used to control whether the DYNAMIC_ADDR is valid or not.
=&gt;When functioning as the Primary Controller, the user/application must set this bit to 1 along with the DYNAMIC_ADDR.
=&gt;When functioning as a Target, the block sets this bit to 1 when the Active Controller assigns the Dynamic address during ENTDAA or SETDASA mechanism. +

Values:
    0x0: Dynamic Address is invalid
    0x1: Dynamic Address is valid</description>
                <bitRange>[31:31]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>COMMAND_QUEUE_PORT</name>
            <description>COMMAND_QUEUE_PORT</description>
            <addressOffset>0xC</addressOffset>
            <access>write-only</access>
            <resetMask>0x00000000</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>COMMAND</name>
                <description>32-bit Command</description>
                <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>RESPONSE_QUEUE_PORT</name>
            <description>RESPONSE_QUEUE_PORT</description>
            <addressOffset>0x10</addressOffset>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>RESPONSE</name>
                <description>32-bit Response</description>
                <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>TX_RX_DATA_PORT</name>
            <description>Transmit/Receive Data Port Register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x14</addressOffset>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>TX_RX_DATA_PORT</name>
                <description>Transmit Data Port for write access and Receive Data Port for read access.

This field is mapped to the TX Data Buffer when writing and to the RX Data Buffer when reading.
The data is always packed in 4-byte aligned data words.
If the command length is not aligned to the 4-bytes, then the additional data bytes have to be ignored.</description>
                <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>IBI_QUEUE_DATA</name>
            <description>In-Band Interrupt Queue Data Register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x18</addressOffset>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>IBI_DATA</name>
                <description>In-Band Interrupt Data

This register is mapped to the IBI Queue.
The IBI Data is always packed in 4-byte aligned and put to the IBI Queue.
If the incoming data is not aligned to the 4-bytes, then there are unused bytes in the end location of the IBI transfer.</description>
                <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>QUEUE_THLD_CTRL</name>
            <description>Queue Threshold Control Register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x1C</addressOffset>
            <resetValue>0x01010101</resetValue>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>CMD_EMPTY_BUF_THLD</name>
                <description>Command Buffer Empty Threshold Value

Controls the minimum number of empty locations in the Command Queue that trigger the CMD_QUEUE_READY_STAT interrupt.
The application should programs only valid values.

Values:
    0x0: Interrupt will trigger when the queue is completely empty
    0x0 &lt; N &lt; 16: Interrupt will trigger when queue has N empty locations.</description>
                <bitRange>[7:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RESP_BUF_THLD</name>
                <description>Response Buffer Threshold Value.

Controls the minimum number of valid entries in the Response Queue that trigger the RESP_READY_STAT_INTR interrupt.
The application should programs only valid values.

Values:
    0x0 &lt;= N &lt; 8: Interrupt will trigger when queue has N+1 valid entries.</description>
                <bitRange>[15:8]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>IBI_DATA_THLD</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[23:16]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>IBI_STATUS_THLD</name>
                <description>In-Band Interrupt Status Threshold Value.

This field controls the number of IBI status entries (or greater) in the IBI queue that trigger the IBI_THLD_STAT interrupt.
Every In Band Interrupt received (with or without Payload) by the block generates an IBI status.
The application should program only valid values.

NOTE: The valid value is only 0 if IBI with payload is selected in the configuration.

Values:
    0x0 &lt;= N &lt; 16: Interrupt will trigger when queue has N+1 valid entries.</description>
                <bitRange>[31:24]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DATA_BUFFER_THLD_CTRL</name>
            <description>Data Buffer Threshold Control Register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x20</addressOffset>
            <resetValue>0x01010101</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x07070707</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>TX_EMPTY_BUF_THLD</name>
                <description>Transmit Buffer Empty Threshold Value

This field controls the number of empty locations (or above) in the TX FIFO that trigger the TX_THLD_STAT interrupt.
If the programmed value is greater than the buffer depth, then threshold is set to IC_TX_BUF_DEPTH.

Supported values:
    0x0: Interrupt triggers with at least 1 empty entry
    0x1: Interrupt triggers with at least 4 empty entries
    0x2: Interrupt triggers with at least 8 empty entries
    0x3: Interrupt triggers with at least 16 empty entries
    0x4: Interrupt triggers with at least 32 empty entries
    0x5: Interrupt triggers with at least 64 empty entries</description>
                <bitRange>[2:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RX_BUF_THLD</name>
                <description>Receive Buffer Threshold Value.

This field controls the number of valid entries (or above) in the RX FIFO that trigger the RX_THLD_STAT interrupt.
If the programmed value is greater than the buffer depth, then threshold is set to IC_RX_BUF_DEPTH.

Supported values:
    0x0: Interrupt triggers with at least 1 valid entry
    0x1: Interrupt triggers with at least 4 valid entries
    0x2: Interrupt triggers with at least 8 valid entries
    0x3: Interrupt triggers with at least 16 valid entries
    0x4: Interrupt triggers with at least 32 valid entries
    0x5: Interrupt triggers with at least  64 valid entries</description>
                <bitRange>[10:8]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TX_START_THLD</name>
                <description>Transfer Start Threshold Value.

When functioning as the Active Controller, when the Controller is set up to initiate a write transfer, it waits until either one of the following conditions are met before it initiates the write transfer on the I3C Interface.
=&gt; Data length (as specified in the command) number of locations are filled in the TX FIFO.
=&gt; Threshold number of entries (or more) are available in the TX FIFO

When functioning as a Target, the Target ACKs a read request from the Active Controller only if either one of the following conditions are met.
=&gt; Data length (as specified in the command) number of locations are filled in the Transmit FIFO
=&gt; Threshold number of entries (or more) are available in the Transmit FIFO

Supported values:
    0x0: Threshold of at least 1 valid entry
    0x1: Threshold of at least 4 valid entries
    0x2: Threshold of at least 8 valid entries
    0x3: Threshold of at least 16 valid entries
    0x4: Threshold of at least 32 valid entries
    0x5: Threshold of at least  64 valid entries</description>
                <bitRange>[18:16]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RX_START_THLD</name>
                <description>Receive Start Threshold Value.

When functioning as the Active Controller, when the controller is set up to initiate a read transfer, it waits until either one of the conditions are met before it initiates the read transfer on the I3C Interface.
=&gt; Data length (as specified in the command) number of locations are empty in the Receive FIFO.
=&gt; Threshold number of locations (or more) are empty in the Receive FIFO.

When functioning as a Target, the block ACKs a write request from the Active Controller only if threshold number of empty locations(or more) are available in its receive buffer.

The supported values for RX_START_THLD are:
    0x0: Threshold of at least 1 empty entry
    0x1: Threshold of at least 4 empty entries
    0x2: Threshold of at least 8 empty entries
    0x3: Threshold of at least 16 empty entries
    0x4: Threshold of at least 32 empty entries
    0x5: Threshold of at least 64 empty entries</description>
                <bitRange>[26:24]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>IBI_QUEUE_CTRL</name>
            <description>IBI Queue Control Register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x24</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x0000000B</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>NOTIFY_HJ_REJECTED</name>
                <description>Notify Rejected Hot-Join Control.

This bit is only valid when this I3C Device is functioning the Active Controller, and is used to suppress reporting to the application about Hot-Join request rejected (NACK and Auto Disable).

Values:
    0x0: Disable passing the IBI Status to the IBI FIFO (hence not notifying the application) when a HJ Request is NACKed and auto-disabled based on the DEVICE_CTRL.HOT_JOIN_CTRL.
    0x1: Enable writing the IBI Status to the IBI FIFO (hence notifying the application) when a HJ Request is NACKed and auto-disabled based on the DEVICE_CTRL.HOT_JOIN_CTRL.</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>NOTIFY_CR_REJECTED</name>
                <description>Notify Rejected Controller Role Request (CR) Control.

This bit is only valid when this I3C Device is functioning the Active Controller, and is used to suppress reporting to the application about Controller Role Request (CR) rejected.

Values:
    0x0: Disable passing the IBI Status to the IBI FIFO (hence not notifying the application) when a CR Request is NACKed and auto-disabled based on the IBI_CR_REQ_REJECT Register.
    0x1: Enable writing the IBI Status to the IBI FIFO (hence notifying the application) when a CR Request is NACKed and auto-disabled based on the IBI_CR_REQ_REJECT Register.</description>
                <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>NOTIFY_TIR_REJECTED</name>
                <description>Notify Rejected Target Interrupt Request Control.

This bit is only valid when this I3C Device is functioning the Active Controller, and is used to suppress reporting to the application about rejected Target Interrupt Request.

Values:
    0x0: Disables passing the IBI Status to the IBI FIFO (hence not notifying the application) when a Target Interrupt Request is NACKed and auto-disabled based on the IBI_TIR_REQ_REJECT Register.
    0x1: Enables writing the  IBI Status to the IBI FIFO (hence notifying the application) when a Target Interrupt Request is NACKed and auto-disabled based on the IBI_TIR_REQ_REJECT Register.</description>
                <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>RESET_CTRL</name>
            <description>Reset Control Register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x34</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0xF000003F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>SOFT_RST</name>
                <description>Core Software Reset.

Write 0x1 to this bit to exercise software reset.
This will trigger an internal procedure that resets all Buffers - RX, TX, CMD, and Response.
This bit is cleared automatically once the core reset is completed.
Before the reset is completed, write accesses and read accesses to the other registers will return a bus error.</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CMD_QUEUE_RST</name>
                <description>Command Queue Software Reset.

Write 0x1 to this bit to exercise Command Queue reset.

This bit is cleared automatically once the Command Queue reset is completed.
Before the reset is completed, write accesses and read accesses to the other registers will return a bus error.</description>
                <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RESP_QUEUE_RST</name>
                <description>Response Queue Software Reset.

Write 1'b1 to this bit to exercise Response Queue reset.

This bit is cleared automatically once the Response Queue reset is completed.
Before the reset is completed, write accesses and read accesses to the other registers will return a bus error.</description>
                <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TX_FIFO_RST</name>
                <description>Transmit Buffer Software Reset.

Write 0x1 to this bit to exercise Transmit Buffer reset.

This bit is cleared automatically once the Transmit Buffer reset is completed.
Before the reset is completed, write accesses and read accesses to the other registers will return a bus error.</description>
                <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RX_FIFO_RST</name>
                <description>Receive Buffer Software Reset.

Write 0x1 to this bit to exercise Receive Buffer reset

This bit is cleared automatically once the Receive buffer reset is completed.
Before the reset is completed, write accesses and read accesses to the other registers will return a bus error.</description>
                <bitRange>[4:4]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>IBI_QUEUE_RST</name>
                <description>IBI Queue Software Reset.

This bit is only valid when functioning as the Active Controller.

Write 0x1 to this bit to exercise IBI Queue reset. This bit is cleared automatically once the IBI Queue reset is completed.
Before the reset is completed, write accesses and read accesses to the other registers will return a bus error.</description>
                <bitRange>[5:5]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BUS_RESET_TYPE</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[30:28]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BUS_RESET</name>
                <description>Write 1'b1 to this bit to exercise Bus Reset Reset Pattern Generation based on Bus Reset Type selection.
This bit is cleared automatically once the Bus Reset Pattern Generation is completed.</description>
                <bitRange>[31:31]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>TGT_EVENT_STATUS</name>
            <description>Target Event Status Register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x38</addressOffset>
            <resetValue>0x0000000B</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x000000FB</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>TIR_EN</name>
                <description>Target Interrupt Request Enable.

This bit is only valid when this I3C Device is functioning as a Target, and reflects whether the block can initiate the TIR on the I3C bus or not.

Usually, this bit is set or cleared by the Active Controller through ENEC or DISEC CCC.</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CR_EN</name>
                <description>Controller Role Request (CR) Enable.

This bit is only valid when this I3C Device is functioning as a Target, and reflects whether the controller can initiate the Controller Role Request (CR) on the I3C bus or not.

Usually, this bit is set or cleared by the Active Controller through ENEC or DISEC CCC.</description>
                <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>HJ_EN</name>
                <description>Hot-Join Interrupt Enable.

This bit is only valid when this I3C Device is functioning as a Target, and reflects whether the Hot-Join Request Interrupts are allowed on the I3C bus or not.

The Target application can choose to Disable HJ Capability of the Target controller (if selected) by setting this field to 0 before 'Enabling' the block. When done so, the Target will not initiate Hot Join and will take part in Address Assignment without initiating Hot Join. If this field is NOT set to 0 by the Target application, it can be set or cleared by the I3C Controller via ENEC or DISEC CCCs. Once Disabled by software, CCCs do not have any effect on this field.</description>
                <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>ACTIVITY_STATE</name>
                <description>Activity State Status

This bit reflects the Activity State of this Target set by the Active Controller.

Values:
    0x0: ENTAS0
    0x1: ENTAS1
    0x2: ENTAS2
    0x3: ENTAS3</description>
                <bitRange>[5:4]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>MRL_UPDATED</name>
                <description>MRL Updated Status.

This bit indicates a SETMRL CCC is received by the Target. The updated MRL value can be read from TGT_MAX_LEN register.

This status can be cleared by writing 0x1 to this field after reading the updated MRL.</description>
                <bitRange>[6:6]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>MWL_UPDATED</name>
                <description>MWL Updated Status.

This bit indicates a SETMWL CCC is received by the Target. The updated MWL value can be read from TGT_MAX_LEN register.

This status can be cleared by writing 0x1 to this field after reading the updated MWL.</description>
                <bitRange>[7:7]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>INTR_STATUS</name>
            <description>Interrupt Status Register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x3C</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x001BBF7F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>TX_THLD_STS</name>
                <description>Transmit Buffer Threshold Status.

This interrupt is generated when the number of empty locations in the transmit buffer is greater than or equal to the threshold value specified by the TX_EMPTY_BUF_THLD field in the DATA_BUFFER_THLD_CTRL register.
This interrupt is cleared automatically when the number of empty locations in transmit buffer is less than threshold value specified.</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RX_THLD_STS</name>
                <description>Receive Buffer Threshold Status.

This interrupt is generated when number of entries in the receive buffer is greater than or equal to threshold value specified by the RX_BUF_THLD field in the DATA_BUFFER_THLD_CTRL register.
This interrupt is cleared automatically when the number of entries in receive buffer is less than the threshold value specified.</description>
                <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>IBI_THLD_STS</name>
                <description>IBI Buffer Threshold Status.

This field is only used when the block is functioning as the Active Controller.

This interrupt is generated when the number of entries in the IBI buffer is greater than or equal to the threshold value specified by the IBI_BUF_THLD field in the QUEUE_THLD_CTRL register.
This interrupt is cleared automatically when the number of entries in IBI buffer is less than the threshold value specified.</description>
                <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CMD_QUEUE_READY_STS</name>
                <description>Command Queue Ready.

This interrupt is generated when number of empty locations in the command queue is greater than or equal to the threshold value specified by the CMD_EMPTY_BUF_THLD field in the QUEUE_THLD_CTRL register.
This interrupt is cleared automatically when the number of empty locations in the command buffer is less than the threshold value specified.</description>
                <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RESP_READY_STS</name>
                <description>Response Queue Ready Status.

This interrupt is generated when number of entries in the response queue is greater than or equal to the threshold value specified by the RESP_BUF_THLD field in the QUEUE_THLD_CTRL register.
This interrupt is cleared automatically when the number of entries in the response buffer is less than the threshold value specified.</description>
                <bitRange>[4:4]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TRANSFER_ABORT_STS</name>
                <description>Transfer Abort Status.

This field is used only when functioning as the Active Controller,.

This interrupt is generated if the transfer is aborted.
This interrupt can be cleared by writing 0x1.</description>
                <bitRange>[5:5]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CCC_UPDATED_STS</name>
                <description>CCC Table Updated Status.

This field is used only when functioning as a Target.

This interrupt is generated if any of the CCC registers are updated by the I3C Controller through CCC commands.
This interrupt can be cleared by writing 0x1.</description>
                <bitRange>[6:6]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>DYN_ADDR_ASSGN_STS</name>
                <description>Dynamic Address Assigned Status

This field is used only in Target mode of operation.
This interrupt is generated when the device's Dynamic Address is assigned through SETDASA, SETAASA, SETNEWDA or ENTDAA CCC.
This bit can be cleared by writing 0x1</description>
                <bitRange>[8:8]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TRANSFER_ERR_STS</name>
                <description>Transfer Error Status.

This interrupt is generated if any error occurs during transfer.
The error type is specified in the response packet associated with the command (in ERR_STATUS field of RESPONSE_QUEUE_PORT register).
This bit can be cleared by writing 0x1.</description>
                <bitRange>[9:9]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>DEFTGT_STS</name>
                <description>Define Target CCC Received Status.

This interrupt is generated if DEFTGTS CCC is received.

This bit can be cleared by writing 0x1.</description>
                <bitRange>[10:10]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>READ_REQ_RECV_STS</name>
                <description>Read Request Received.

This field is used only when functioning as a Target.

Read Request received from the Active Controller when CMDQ is empty.
This bit can be cleared by writing 0x1.</description>
                <bitRange>[11:11]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>IBI_UPDATED_STS</name>
                <description>IBI status is updated.

This field is used only when functioning as a Target.

It indicates that the IBI request initiated through TIR request register is addressed and status is updated.</description>
                <bitRange>[12:12]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BUSOWNER_UPDATED_STS</name>
                <description>This interrupt is set when the block changes from being the Active Controller to a Secondary Controller/Target or vice versa.

This bit can be cleared by writing 0x1.</description>
                <bitRange>[13:13]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BUS_RESET_DONE_STS</name>
                <description>Bus Reset Pattern Generation Done Status

This field is used only when functioning as a Controller.

This interrupt is generated when the requested Bus Reset Pattern Generation is completed.
This bit can be cleared by writing 1'b1.</description>
                <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>START_DET_STS</name>
                <description>START Detection Interrupt.

This field is used only when functioning as a Target and indicates that a START has been detected.

This interrupt is provided for the user/application (in CCR mode) to decide whether the NCR has taken over the ownership after the bus handover was completed successfully to move to Target mode.
And to reset the reset action configured by the RSTACT CCC.

This bit can be cleared by writing 1'b1.</description>
                <bitRange>[16:16]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TGT_RST_PATTERN_DET_STS</name>
                <description>Target Reset Pattern Detection Interrupt

This field is used only when functioning as a Target.

This interrupt is generated when the Target Reset pattern is detected.</description>
                <bitRange>[17:17]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SDA_RELEASED_STS</name>
                <description>SDA Released from Stuck state Status.
This field is used only in target mode of operation.
This interrupt is generated if the SCL was not changed for the
prescribed amount of time defined by
RELEASE_SDA_TIMING register.
This bit can be cleared by writing 1'b1</description>
                <bitRange>[19:19]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EXT_CMD_SENT_STS</name>
                <description>EXT_CMD_REG_x has finished Status.
This field is used only in target mode of operation.
This interrupt is generated if Read Transfer has finished in the
EXT_CMD_REG_x registers.
This interrupt is cleared automatically when register
EXT_CMD_VLD_STAT is read</description>
                <bitRange>[20:20]</bitRange>
                <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>INTR_STATUS_EN</name>
            <description>Interrupt Status Enable Register.</description>
            <addressOffset>0x40</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x001BBF7F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>TX_THLD_STS_EN</name>
                <description>Transmit Buffer Threshold Status Enable</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RX_THLD_STS_EN</name>
                <description>Receive Buffer Threshold Status Enable</description>
                <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>IBI_THLD_STS_EN</name>
                <description>IBI Buffer Threshold Status Enable

This field is only valid when functioning aa the Active Controller.</description>
                <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CMD_QUEUE_READY_STS_EN</name>
                <description>Command Queue Ready Status Enable</description>
                <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RESP_READY_STS_EN</name>
                <description>Response Queue Ready Status Enable</description>
                <bitRange>[4:4]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TRANSFER_ABORT_STS_EN</name>
                <description>Transfer Abort Status Enable.

This field is only valid when functioning as the Active Controller.</description>
                <bitRange>[5:5]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CCC_UPDATED_STS_EN</name>
                <description>CCC Table Updated Status Enable.

This field is only valid when functioning as a Target.</description>
                <bitRange>[6:6]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>DYN_ADDR_ASSGN_STS_EN</name>
                <description>Dynamic Address Assigned Status Enable

This field is only valid when functioning as a Target.</description>
                <bitRange>[8:8]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TRANSFER_ERR_STS_EN</name>
                <description>Transfer Error Status Enable</description>
                <bitRange>[9:9]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>DEFTGT_STS_EN</name>
                <description>Define Target CCC Received Status Enable</description>
                <bitRange>[10:10]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>READ_REQ_RECV_STS_EN</name>
                <description>Read Request Received Status Enable

This field is only valid when functioning as a Target.</description>
                <bitRange>[11:11]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>IBI_UPDATED_STS_EN</name>
                <description>IBI Updated Status Enable

This field is only valid when functioning as a Target.</description>
                <bitRange>[12:12]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BUSOWNER_UPDATED_STS_EN</name>
                <description>Bus owner Updated Status Enable</description>
                <bitRange>[13:13]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BUS_RESET_DONE_STS_EN</name>
                <description>Bus Reset Pattern Generation Done Status Enable</description>
                <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>START_DET_STS_EN</name>
                <description>START Detection Interrupt Status Enable</description>
                <bitRange>[16:16]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TGT_RST_PATTERN_DET_STS_EN</name>
                <description>Target Reset Pattern Detection Interrupt Status Enable</description>
                <bitRange>[17:17]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SDA_RELEASED_STS_EN</name>
                <description>SDA Released from Stuck state Status Enable.
This field is used in target mode of operation</description>
                <bitRange>[19:19]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EXT_CMD_SENT_STS_EN</name>
                <description>EXT_CMD_REG_x has finished Signal Enable.

This field is used when operating as a Target.</description>
                <bitRange>[20:20]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>INTR_SIGNAL_EN</name>
            <description>Interrupt Signal Enable Register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x44</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x001BBF7F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>TX_THLD_SIGNAL_EN</name>
                <description>Transmit Buffer Threshold Signal Enable</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RX_THLD_SIGNAL_EN</name>
                <description>Receive Buffer Threshold Signal Enable</description>
                <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>IBI_THLD_SIGNAL_EN</name>
                <description>IBI Buffer Threshold Signal Enable

This field is only valid when functioning aa the Active Controller.</description>
                <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CMD_QUEUE_READY_SIGNAL_EN</name>
                <description>Command Queue Ready Signal Enable</description>
                <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RESP_READY_SIGNAL_EN</name>
                <description>Response Queue Ready Signal Enable</description>
                <bitRange>[4:4]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TRANSFER_ABORT_SIGNAL_EN</name>
                <description>Transfer Abort Signal Enable.

This field is only valid when functioning as the Active Controller.</description>
                <bitRange>[5:5]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CCC_UPDATED_SIGNAL_EN</name>
                <description>CCC Table Updated Signal Enable.

This field is only valid when functioning as a Target.</description>
                <bitRange>[6:6]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>DYN_ADDR_ASSGN_SIGNAL_EN</name>
                <description>Dynamic Address Assigned Signal Enable

This field is only valid when functioning as a Target.</description>
                <bitRange>[8:8]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TRANSFER_ERR_SIGNAL_EN</name>
                <description>Transfer Error Signal Enable</description>
                <bitRange>[9:9]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>DEFTGT_SIGNAL_EN</name>
                <description>Define Target CCC Received Signal Enable</description>
                <bitRange>[10:10]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>READ_REQ_RECV_SIGNAL_EN</name>
                <description>Read Request Received Signal Enable

This field is only valid when functioning as a Target.</description>
                <bitRange>[11:11]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>IBI_UPDATED_SIGNAL_EN</name>
                <description>IBI Updated Signal Enable

This field is only valid when functioning as a Target.</description>
                <bitRange>[12:12]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BUSOWNER_UPDATED_SIGNAL_EN</name>
                <description>Bus owner Updated Signal Enable</description>
                <bitRange>[13:13]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BUS_RESET_DONE_SIGNAL_EN</name>
                <description>Bus Reset Pattern Generation Done Signal Enable</description>
                <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>START_DET_SIGNAL_EN</name>
                <description>START Detection Interrupt Signal Enable</description>
                <bitRange>[16:16]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TGT_RST_PATTERN_DET_SIGNAL_EN</name>
                <description>Target Reset Pattern Detection Interrupt Signal Enable</description>
                <bitRange>[17:17]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SDA_RELEASED_SIGNAL_EN</name>
                <description>SDA Released from Stuck state Signal Enable.
This field is used in target mode of operation</description>
                <bitRange>[19:19]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EXT_CMD_SENT_SIGNAL_EN</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[20:20]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>INTR_FORCE</name>
            <description>Interrupt Force Enable Register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x48</addressOffset>
            <access>write-only</access>
            <resetMask>0x001BBF7F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>TX_THLD_FORCE_EN</name>
                <description>Transmit Buffer Threshold Force Enable</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RX_THLD_FORCE_EN</name>
                <description>Receive Buffer Threshold Force Enable</description>
                <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>IBI_THLD_FORCE_EN</name>
                <description>IBI Buffer Threshold Force Enable

This field is only valid when functioning aa the Active Controller.</description>
                <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CMD_QUEUE_READY_FORCE_EN</name>
                <description>Command Queue Ready Force Enable</description>
                <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RESP_READY_FORCE_EN</name>
                <description>Response Queue Ready Force Enable</description>
                <bitRange>[4:4]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TRANSFER_ABORT_FORCE_EN</name>
                <description>Transfer Abort Force Enable.

This field is only valid when functioning as the Active Controller.</description>
                <bitRange>[5:5]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CCC_UPDATED_FORCE_EN</name>
                <description>CCC Table Updated Force Enable.

This field is only valid when functioning as a Target.</description>
                <bitRange>[6:6]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>DYN_ADDR_ASSGN_FORCE_EN</name>
                <description>Dynamic Address Assigned Force Enable

This field is only valid when functioning as a Target.</description>
                <bitRange>[8:8]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TRANSFER_ERR_FORCE_EN</name>
                <description>Transfer Error Force Enable</description>
                <bitRange>[9:9]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>DEFTGT_FORCE_EN</name>
                <description>Define Target CCC Received Force Enable</description>
                <bitRange>[10:10]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>READ_REQ_FORCE_EN</name>
                <description>Read Request Received Force Enable

This field is only valid when functioning as a Target.</description>
                <bitRange>[11:11]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>IBI_UPDATED_FORCE_EN</name>
                <description>IBI Updated Force Enable

This field is only valid when functioning as a Target.</description>
                <bitRange>[12:12]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BUSOWNER_UPDATED_FORCE_EN</name>
                <description>Bus owner Updated Force Enable</description>
                <bitRange>[13:13]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BUS_RESET_DONE_FORCE_EN</name>
                <description>Bus Reset Pattern Generation Done force Enable</description>
                <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>START_DET_FORCE_EN</name>
                <description>START Detection Interrupt force Enable</description>
                <bitRange>[16:16]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TGT_RST_PATTERN_DET_FORCE_EN</name>
                <description>Target Reset Pattern Detection Interrupt force Enable</description>
                <bitRange>[17:17]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SDA_RELEASED_FORCE_EN</name>
                <description>SDA Released from Stuck state Force Enable.
This field is used in target mode of operation</description>
                <bitRange>[19:19]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EXT_CMD_SENT_FORCE_EN</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[20:20]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>QUEUE_STATUS_LEVEL</name>
            <description>Queue Status Level Register.</description>
            <addressOffset>0x4C</addressOffset>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetValue>0x00000010</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x1FFFFFFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>CMD_QUEUE_EMPTY_LOC</name>
                <description>Command Queue Empty Locations.

Contains the number of empty locations in the command Buffer.</description>
                <bitRange>[7:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RESP_BUF_BLR</name>
                <description>Response Buffer Level Value.

Contains the number of valid data entries in the response Buffer.</description>
                <bitRange>[15:8]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>IBI_BUF_BLR</name>
                <description>IBI Buffer Level Value.

This field is used when functioning as the Active Controller.

Contains the number of Valid IBI Status entries in the IBI Buffer.</description>
                <bitRange>[23:16]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>IBI_STS_CNT</name>
                <description>&lt;HIDDEN&gt;</description>
                <bitRange>[28:24]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DATA_BUFFER_STATUS_LEVEL</name>
            <description>Data Buffer Status Level Register.</description>
            <addressOffset>0x50</addressOffset>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetValue>0x00000040</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x00FF00FF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>TX_BUF_EMPTY_LOC</name>
                <description>Transmit Buffer Empty Level Value.

Contains the number of empty locations in the transmit Buffer.</description>
                <bitRange>[7:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RX_BUF_BLR</name>
                <description>Receive Buffer Level Value.

Contains the number of valid data entries in the receive Buffer.</description>
                <bitRange>[23:16]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>PRESENT_STATE</name>
            <description>Present State Register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x54</addressOffset>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetValue>0x10000003</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x1F3F3F07</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>SCL_LINE_SIGNAL_LEVEL</name>
                <description>This bit is only valid when this I3C Device is functioning the Active Controller, and is used for debug and error recovery.

This bit reflects the value of the SCL line as observed inside the block (after input synchronization).</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SDA_LINE_SIGNAL_LEVEL</name>
                <description>This bit is only valid when this I3C Device is functioning the Active Controller, and is used for debug and error recovery.

This bit reflects the value of the SDA line as observed inside the block (after input synchronization).</description>
                <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>ACTIVE_CONTROLLER</name>
                <description>This Bit is used to check whether the block is currently the Active Controller or not. The Active Controller is the Controller that owns the SCL line.

Values:
    0x0: Secondary Controller is not the Active Controller and needs to request to be the Active Controller before initiating any transfer on the line.
    0x1: Secondary Controller is the Active Controller and can initiate transfers on the line.</description>
                <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CM_TFR_STS</name>
                <description>Transfer Type Status.

Indicates the type of transfer currently executing by the I3C block.

When functioning as the Active Controller, possible values are:
    0x0: IDLE (Block is in Idle state, waiting for commands from application or Target initated In-band Interrupt)
    0x1: Broadcast CCC Write Transfer.
    0x2: Directed CCC Write Transfer.
    0x3: Directed CCC Read Transfer.
    0x4: ENTDAA Address Assignment Transfer.
    0x5: SETDASA Address Assignment Transfer.
    0x6: Private I3C SDR Write Transfer.
    0x7: Private I3C SDR Read Transfer.
    0x8: Private I2C SDR Write Transfer.
    0x9: Private I2C SDR Read Transfer.
    0xA: Private HDR Ternary Symbol (TS) Write Transfer.
    0xB: Private HDR Ternary Symbol (TS) Read Transfer.
    0xC: Private HDR Double-Data Rate (DDR) Write Transfer.
    0xD: Private HDR Double-Data Rate (DDR) Read Transfer.
    0xE: Servicing In-Band Interrupt Transfer.
    0xF: Halt state (Block is in Halt State, waiting for the application to resume through DEVICE_CTRL Register)

When functioning as a Target, possible values are:
    0x0: IDLE (Block is in Idle state)
    0x1: Hot-Join transfer state
    0x2: IBI transfer state
    0x3: Controller write transfer ongoing
    0x4: Read data prefetch state
    0x5: Controller read transfer ongoing
    0x6: Bock is controller in Halt State waiting for resume from application</description>
                <bitRange>[13:8]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CM_TFR_ST_STS</name>
                <description>Active Controller Transfer State Status.

This bit is only valid when this I3C Device is functioning the Active Controller, and indicates the state of current transfer currently executing by the I3C block.

Values:
    0x0: IDLE (Controller is Idle state, waiting for commands from application or Target initated In-band Interrupt)
    0x1: START Generation State.
    0x2: RESTART Generation State.
    0x3: STOP Generation State.
    0x4: START Hold Generation for the Target Initiated START State.
    0x5: Broadcast Write Address Header(7'h7E,W) Generation State.
    0x6: Broadcast Read Address Header(7'h7E,R) Generation State.
    0x7: Dynamic Address Assignment State.
    0x8: Target Address Generation State.
    0xB: CCC Byte Generation State.
    0xC: HDR Command Generation State.
    0xD: Write Data Transfer State.
    0xE: Read Data Transfer State.
    0xF: In-Band Interrupt (TIR) Read Data State.
    0x10: In-Band Interrupt Auto-Disable State
    0x11: HDR-DDR CRC Data Generation/Receive State.
    0x12: Clock Extension State.
    0x13: Halt State.</description>
                <bitRange>[21:16]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CMD_TID</name>
                <description>This field reflects the Transaction-ID of the current executing command.</description>
                <bitRange>[27:24]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CONTROLLER_IDLE</name>
                <description>This bit is only valid when this I3C Device is functioning the Active Controller, and reflects whether the block is in the Idle state or not.
This bit is set when all the Queues (Command , Response, IBI) and Buffers (Transmit and Receive) are empty and the Controller State machine is in Idle state.

Values:
    0x0 Controller is not in IDLE State
    0x1 Controller is in IDLE State.</description>
                <bitRange>[28:28]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>CCC_DEVICE_STATUS</name>
            <description>Device Operating Status Register.</description>
            <addressOffset>0x58</addressOffset>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetMask>0x00003FEF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>PENDING_INTR</name>
                <description>Pending Interrupt

This field reflects the value from I3C.CCC_GETSTATUS.PENDING_INT.</description>
                <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>PROTOCOL_ERR</name>
                <description>Protocol Error

This bit is only valid when this I3C Device is functioning as a Target, and is set when a Parity/CRC error is encountered during a private write transfer initiated by the Active Controller.</description>
                <bitRange>[5:5]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>ACTIVITY_MODE</name>
                <description>Pending Interrupt

This field reflects the value from I3C.CCC_GETSTATUS.ACT_MODE.</description>
                <bitRange>[7:6]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>UNDERFLOW_ERR</name>
                <description>Underflow error

This bit is only valid when this I3C Device is functioning as a Target, and is set when terminates a private read transfer initiated by the Active Controller is terminated by the device because of unavailability of data in the transmit buffer.
This bit is cleared only after the Active Controller reads the Device Status through a GETSTATUS CCC.</description>
                <bitRange>[8:8]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TARGET_BUSY</name>
                <description>Target Busy

This bit is set if any change is made by the Active Controller to the MRL register or occurance of any error.
It is cleared after Target application resumes the block operation by writing to the RESUME bit of the DEVICE_CONTROL register.</description>
                <bitRange>[9:9]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>OVERFLOW_ERR</name>
                <description>Overflow Error

This bit is only valid when this I3C Device is functioning as a Target, and is set when an overflow error condition is detected during a private write transfer initiated by the Active Controller.
This is cleared only after the Active Controller reads the Device Status through GETSTATUS CCC.</description>
                <bitRange>[10:10]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>DATA_NOT_READY</name>
                <description>Data not ready

This bit is only valid when this I3C Device is functioning as a Target, and is set when private a private read request initiated by the Active Controller is NACKED because of any of the following conditions
    Command FIFO Empty.
    Transmit FIFO threshold is not met.
    Response FIFO Full.

This bit is cleared when the Active Controller issues GETSTATUS CCC or upon successful completion of the subsequent read transfer.</description>
                <bitRange>[11:11]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BUFFER_NOT_AVAIL</name>
                <description>Buffer not available

This bit is only valid when this I3C Device is functioning as a Target, and is set when a private write request initiated by the Active Controller is NACKED because of RX buffer not having RX_BUF_THLD number of empty locations or Response buffer being full.
In SDR mode of operation this is cleared when the Active Controller issues GETSTATUS CCC or upon space becoming available in the buffer and the successful completion of the next write transfer.
In HDR mode of operation it is cleared only when the Active Controller issues GETSTATUS CCC.</description>
                <bitRange>[12:12]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>FRAME_ERROR</name>
                <description>Frame Error

This bit is only valid when this I3C Device is functioning as a Target, and is set when a private write request initiated by the Active Controller has a frame error in HDR-DDR/HDR-TSP/TSL mode.
This is cleared only after the Active Controller reads the device status through GETSTATUS_CCC.</description>
                <bitRange>[13:13]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DEVICE_ADDR_TABLE_POINTER</name>
            <description>Pointer for Device Address Table</description>
            <addressOffset>0x5C</addressOffset>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetValue>0x000B02C0</resetValue>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>P_DEV_ADDR_TABLE_START_ADDR</name>
                <description>Start Address of the Device Address Table in the internal memory.</description>
                <bitRange>[15:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>DEV_ADDR_TABLE_DEPTH</name>
                <description>Depth of the Device Address Table in the internal memory.</description>
                <bitRange>[31:16]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DEV_CHAR_TABLE_POINTER</name>
            <description>Pointer for Device Characteristics Table</description>
            <addressOffset>0x60</addressOffset>
            <resetValue>0x0002C200</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x007FFFFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>P_DEV_CHAR_TABLE_START_ADDR</name>
                <description>Start Address of the Device Characteristics Table in the internal memory.</description>
                <bitRange>[11:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>DEV_CHAR_TABLE_DEPTH</name>
                <description>Depth of Device Characteristics Table in the internal memory.</description>
                <bitRange>[18:12]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>PRESENT_DEV_CHAR_TABLE_INDX</name>
                <description>Current index of the Device Characteristics Table.

This field returns the current location of Device Characteristics Table index. Initially, this index points to 0.

Once the complete characteristics information of a Target device is written into Device Characteristics Table during ENTDAA, this index increments by 1. The first winning device information is stored in Device Characteristics Table index 0, the second winning device information in index 1, and so on.

If required, this index can be used to override the location, where characteristic information of Target devices on the I3C bus are written during ENTDAA. Hence, this field is useful only when the device is functioning as the Active Controller. During DEFTGTS CCC, the index always starts from 0.

When the device is functioning as a Target, this field is read-only.</description>
                <bitRange>[22:19]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>VENDOR_SPECIFIC_REG_POINTER</name>
            <description>Pointer for Vendor Specific Registers.</description>
            <addressOffset>0x6C</addressOffset>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetValue>0x000000B0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x0000FFFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>P_VENDOR_REG_START_ADDR</name>
                <description>Start Address of Vendor specific registers.</description>
                <bitRange>[15:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>TGT_MIPI_ID_VALUE</name>
            <description>I3C  MIPI Manufacturer ID Register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x70</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x0000FFFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>TGT_PROV_ID_SEL</name>
                <description>Specifies the Provisioned ID Type Selector (Provisioned ID [32]).

Values:
    0x0: Vendor Fixed Value
    0x1: Random Value</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TGT_MIPI_MFG_ID</name>
                <description>Specifies the MIPI Manufacturer ID (Provisioned ID [47:33]).</description>
                <bitRange>[15:1]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>TGT_PID_VALUE</name>
            <description>I3C Normal Provisional ID Register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x74</addressOffset>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>TGT_PID_DCR</name>
                <description>Specifies the last 12 bit of the MIPI Provisioned ID of this I3C block (Provisioned ID  [11:0]).</description>
                <bitRange>[11:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TGT_INST_ID</name>
                <description>Specifies the MIPI Instance ID of this I3C block (Provisioned ID  [15:12]).</description>
                <bitRange>[15:12]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TGT_PART_ID</name>
                <description>Specifies the Part ID of this I3C block (PID[31:16]).</description>
                <bitRange>[31:16]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>TGT_CHAR_CTRL</name>
            <description>I3C Target Characteristic Register.</description>
            <addressOffset>0x78</addressOffset>
            <resetValue>0x00010067</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x00FFFFFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>MAX_DATA_SPEED_LIMIT</name>
                <description>Max Data Speed Limitation field in Bus Characteristic Register (BCR[0]). Specifies whether or not this I3C device has a maximum data speed limitation.

If this bit is set to 0, controller NACK's the GETMXDS CCC sent by the Active Controller.
If this bit is set to 1, controller returns the data in MAX_DATA_SPEED and MAX_READ_TURNAROUND register in response the GETMXDS CCC sent by the Active Controller.

The field is reset to its default value upon HW/SW Reset. SW must program the values again after issuing reset.</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>IBI_REQUEST_CAPABLE</name>
                <description>&lt;HIDDEN&gt;</description>
                <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>IBI_PAYLOAD</name>
                <description>&lt;HIDDEN&gt;</description>
                <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>OFFLINE_CAPABLE</name>
                <description>&lt;HIDDEN&gt;</description>
                <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BRIDGE_IDENTIFIER</name>
                <description>&lt;HIDDEN&gt;</description>
                <bitRange>[4:4]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>HDR_CAPABLE</name>
                <description>SDR Only or SDR and HDR Capable field in Bus Characteristic Register (BCR[5]).

This bit can be modified by the application if it does not want to advertize the Target's HDR capability to the Active Controller.

The field is Reset to its default value upon HW/SW Reset.
SW must program the values again after issuing reset.</description>
                <bitRange>[5:5]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>DEVICE_ROLE</name>
                <description>Device Role field in Bus Characteristic Register (BCR[7:6]).

This bit is set by default as 0x1 (device has Controller capabilities) and the application is not expected to change it.
However, if the application chooses to operate the device as a Target-only device (no controlelr capability), then the Device Role can be overwritten as Target-only.

Values:
    0x0: I3C Target
    0x1: I3C Controller capable

The field is Reset to its default value upon HW/SW Reset.
SW must program the values again after issuing reset.</description>
                <bitRange>[7:6]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TGT_DCR</name>
                <description>I3C Device Characteristic Regster (DCR) Value</description>
                <bitRange>[15:8]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>HDR_CAP</name>
                <description>&lt;HIDDEN&gt;</description>
                <bitRange>[23:16]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>TGT_MAX_LEN</name>
            <description>I3C Max Write/Read Length Register.</description>
            <addressOffset>0x7C</addressOffset>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetValue>0x00FF00FF</resetValue>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>MWL</name>
                <description>I3C Device Max Write Length</description>
                <bitRange>[15:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>MRL</name>
                <description>I3C Device Max Read Length</description>
                <bitRange>[31:16]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>MAX_READ_TURNAROUND</name>
            <description>MXDS Maximum Read Turnaround Time.</description>
            <addressOffset>0x80</addressOffset>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetMask>0x00FFFFFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>MXDS_MAX_RD_TURN</name>
                <description>Specifies the maximum read turnaround time (in microseconds (us)) of this I3C device.</description>
                <bitRange>[23:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>MAX_DATA_SPEED</name>
            <description>Maximum Data Speed Register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x84</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x070F070F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>MXDS_MAX_WR_SPEED</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[2:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>MXDS_DEF_BYTE_SUPPORT</name>
                <description>Specifies the defining byte support of GETMXDS CCC                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Values:
- 0x0 (DEF_BYTE_0): Defining byte is not supported for
GETMXDS CCC.
- 0x1 (DEF_BYTE_1): Defining byte is supported for
GETMXDS CCC</description>
                <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>MXDS_MAX_RD_SPEED</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[10:8]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>MXDS_CLK_DATA_TURN</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[18:16]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>MXDS_STOP_PERMIT</name>
                <description>Specifies the stop support between write and read                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             Values:
- 0x0 (STOP_PERMIT_0): Stop after write will cancel the
read.
- 0x1 (STOP_PERMIT_1): Target permits the write and read
to be seperated by a STOP</description>
                <bitRange>[19:19]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>MXDS_ACTIVITY_STATE</name>
                <description>This 2-bit integer indicates whether this I3C Device initially  acts with a given Activity State, after becoming Active  Controller on the Bus according to the Controller handoff flow                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Values:
- 0x0 (ACIVITY_STATE_0): Acts as Activity state 0.
- 0x1 (ACIVITY_STATE_1): Acts as Activity state 1.
- 0x2 (ACIVITY_STATE_2): Acts as Activity state 2.
- 0x3 (ACIVITY_STATE_3): Acts as Activity state 3</description>
                <bitRange>[25:24]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>MXDS_SET_ACTIVITY_STATE</name>
                <description>This bit indicates whether the Active Controller should set the  Bus to the Activity State indicated in bits 25:24 before handing  the Controller Role off to this I3C Device as part of the normal flow.
Values:
- 0x0 (SET_ACIVITY_STATE_0): The controller will not set
the Bus to any Activity state.
- 0x1 (SET_ACIVITY_STATE_1): The controller will set the
Bus to any Activity state based on the value in 25:24 field
of register</description>
                <bitRange>[26:26]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>TGT_INTR_REQ</name>
            <description>Target Interrupt Request Register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x8C</addressOffset>
            <resetMask>0x00FFFF9F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>TIR</name>
                <description>Target Interrupt Request

When set, the Target Controller attempts to issue the TIR on the I3C bus.
Once issued and when the Active Controller accepts (ACK), or if the block is unable to issue the TIR, then the block clears this bit automatically and updates the IBI_STS field.
If the NACK response is received for the TIR, the block reattempts the TIR upon detecting the next START condition from the Active Controller or after the Bus Available Time.

Once set, the application cannot clear this bit.</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TIR_CTRL</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[2:1]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CR</name>
                <description>Controller Role Request

When set, the block attempts to issue the request to become the Active Controller on the I3C bus.
Once that is issued and the Active Controller accepts (ACK), or if the block is unable to issue the request, then the block clears this bit automatically and updates the IBI_STS field.
If NACK response is received for the request, the block reattempts the CR upon detecting the next START condition from the Active Controller or after the Bus Available time.

Once set, the application cannot clear this bit.</description>
                <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TGT_TS</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[4:4]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CE3_RECOVERY</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[7:7]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>MDB</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[15:8]</bitRange>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TIR_DATA_LENGTH</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[23:16]</bitRange>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>TGT_TIR_DATA</name>
